/* English counterparts of the original Chinese study material. Canonical IDs and option order are unchanged. */
window.PMP_EN = window.PMP_EN || {questions:{},lessons:{},weeks:{},sources:{}};
(() => {
const questions = {};
const put = (id,topic,difficulty,stem,options,explanation,eliminations) => { questions[id]={topic,difficulty,stem,options,explanation,eliminations:eliminations.map(x=>x===null?explanation:x)}; };
put('a001','Team conflict','Foundation','Two senior engineers disagree over the system architecture. Both proposals could meet the requirements, and delivery is now being affected. What should the project manager do first?',[
'Clarify shared objectives, constraints, and evaluation criteria, then help both engineers compare the proposals against them.',
'Ask an independent architect to select the strongest technical solution, then require both engineers to implement it.',
'Have each engineer build a prototype and use measured performance as the sole basis for the final choice.',
'Let the sponsor choose the solution so the team can focus on implementation and restore delivery.'
],'The disagreement concerns technical trade-offs, so facts and shared criteria should guide the discussion. A collaborative resolution produces a workable choice while retaining both engineers’ commitment.',[null,'Independent review may help, but shared constraints and criteria have not yet been established. Technical preference could be mistaken for the best project decision.','Prototypes can provide evidence, but performance may not be the only criterion. Building them before clarifying the trade-offs can waste resources.','The professional disagreement can still be resolved at team level. Immediate escalation bypasses necessary joint analysis.']);
put('a002','Servant leadership','Foundation','For two consecutive iterations, approvals for a test environment have delayed the development team. The team has repeatedly contacted the approving department without success. What should the agile project leader do?',[
'Replace the waiting test tasks with development tasks to increase the amount completed in the current iteration.',
'Help resolve the cross-department approval impediment while preserving the team’s autonomy over development work.',
'Coach the team to update its impediment log and devise another contact approach at the next retrospective.',
'Take over test scheduling and approval contacts, allocating all related work centrally from now on.'
],'Servant leadership includes removing impediments beyond the team’s influence. The team has already tried to resolve the issue itself; the leader should provide organizational support without taking over daily work allocation.',['Rearranging tasks may reduce waiting temporarily, but it leaves the approval bottleneck in place and can accumulate unverified work.',null,'Repeated unsuccessful attempts indicate a need for cross-department support, not simply another internal improvement cycle.','The leader should address the external impediment, but taking over all internal arrangements goes beyond what is needed.']);
put('a003','Psychological safety','Foundation','A new team member never speaks during retrospectives. Privately, the member says they have spotted several defects but fear ridicule from senior colleagues. How should the leader improve the situation?',[
'Collect the new member’s concerns privately before the next retrospective and raise them on the member’s behalf.',
'Arrange presentation-skills training so the new member feels more confident speaking in front of senior colleagues.',
'Agree on respectful, blame-free learning norms with the team and introduce safe participation methods such as round-robin input.',
'Replace retrospectives with anonymous surveys, then have only senior members discuss the collected issues.'
],'First create an environment where people can report problems. Explicit interaction norms and room for different voices address the team-level cause more effectively than expecting the new member alone to become braver.',['This may provide temporary support, but continued proxy speaking does not improve interaction safety or replace shared norms.','Training assumes the problem is the new member’s speaking ability rather than addressing the team climate behind the concern.',null,'Anonymous input can help, but restricting discussion to senior members still limits the new member’s participation and learning.']);
put('a004','Virtual teams','Foundation','A project team works across Toronto, Singapore, and London. All synchronous meetings are held in the Toronto morning, and participation from Singapore is declining. What is the best adjustment?',[
'Keep the fixed meeting time, send recordings to absent members, and require their feedback the next day.',
'Combine all meetings into one long weekly session at the time most convenient for headquarters.',
'Have regional leads attend and relay all technical decisions through their respective reporting structures.',
'Agree on documentation and response rules for asynchronous communication, and rotate necessary live meeting times.'
],'Distributed collaboration requires a fair sharing of time-zone inconvenience. Asynchronous records reduce unnecessary meetings, while rotating important live sessions supports participation and sustainable teamwork.',['Recordings help but do not share the ongoing burden of late-night attendance. Important decisions may still be made without these members.','Lower frequency may reduce some burden, but the same region still bears the unfavorable time slot for a long meeting.','Representation suits some matters, but routing all technical collaboration through leads can increase information loss and waiting.',null]);
put('a005','Cultural differences','Advanced','A team member usually remains silent during meetings but sends detailed objections afterward. Other members consider this uncooperative. What should the project manager do first?',[
'Privately learn about the member’s communication preferences, then agree on participation options such as written feedback before meetings.',
'Have a senior colleague coach the member before meetings so they adapt to the existing speaking rules.',
'Treat every post-meeting comment as a formal change and require the member to justify not speaking earlier.',
'Add a cultural-awareness presentation for the team, then continue using the existing meeting arrangements.'
],'Do not infer motives from behavior alone. Understanding cultural or personal communication preferences can reveal ways, such as advance materials or written feedback, for different styles to contribute to decisions.',[null,'Placing the burden of change on the individual before understanding the cause may overlook improvements to the communication process.','Applying change control before understanding the communication style and decision status can turn useful participation into a punishment.','General training may help, but it does not replace learning this member’s actual preferences and the team’s interaction needs.']);
put('a006','Stakeholder engagement','Foundation','A system is about to go live when the operations manager says they have never seen the requirements and refuses to accept it. Records show that operations was omitted from the stakeholder list. What is the best next action?',[
'Ask the sponsor to reaffirm the go-live decision, then invite operations to the formal handover meeting.',
'Understand operations’ handover needs and concerns, update the engagement analysis, and assess delivery impacts.',
'Have developers demonstrate the existing functionality to prove that the system meets the original requirements.',
'Delay go-live by one month so operations can thoroughly inspect the system and submit additional requirements.'
],'First obtain the critical perspective that was missed, then assess the necessary action. Stakeholder identification is ongoing; an existing plan cannot substitute for real engagement.',['Reaffirming the original decision does not address omitted operational needs. Understand the acceptance obstacles first.',null,'A demonstration can aid communication, but it still centers on existing requirements without establishing operations’ acceptance conditions.','Whether to delay, and for how long, should follow an impact assessment rather than precede understanding the problem.']);
put('a007','Team charter','Foundation','A newly formed cross-functional team has no shared expectations for response times, meeting conduct, or conflict resolution. What would best help establish common agreements?',[
'Adopt the working agreement from a successful company project and tell members to follow it at kickoff.',
'Let functional managers set their own departmental rules, with the project manager coordinating daily exceptions.',
'Facilitate a team-created working agreement that defines collaboration expectations and is reviewed regularly.',
'Complete a detailed responsibility assignment matrix first and defer communication behavior to performance reviews.'
],'Working agreements turn behavioral expectations into rules that members jointly accept. Participation increases commitment, and the agreement should evolve as the team gains experience.',['A proven template is a useful starting point, but the team must tailor it and develop shared understanding.','Separate rules preserve interface differences and leave repeated exceptions for the project manager to resolve.',null,'A responsibility matrix clarifies duties but does not replace interaction agreements about meetings, responses, and conflict.']);
put('a008','Boundaries of empowerment','Advanced','A self-managing team wants to choose its own open-source component. One candidate has a license prohibited by organizational policy. How should the project leader respond?',[
'Have legal approve every candidate, then let the project manager make the final technical selection.',
'Record the license as a technical risk and allow a trial before requesting permission for production use.',
'Ask procurement to find a commercial replacement and replace all open-source candidates immediately.',
'Clarify the license-compliance constraints and let the team choose independently among compliant alternatives.'
],'Empowerment does not remove constraints. The leader should make non-negotiable organizational boundaries clear while preserving the team’s authority to choose within those boundaries.',['Legal can confirm the boundaries, but this does not require transferring all compliant technical choices back to the manager.','The license is already known to be prohibited. Recording a risk does not grant permission to use it.','The restriction applies to one candidate’s license, not to all open-source components.',null]);
put('a009','Performance coaching','Foundation','A previously reliable team member has frequently missed delivery commitments over the past two weeks. The project manager does not yet know why. What is the best first step?',[
'Hold a private discussion using specific examples, understand the obstacles, and agree on improvement actions.',
'Assign an experienced colleague to review all of the member’s work to protect the current milestone first.',
'Raise the member’s performance targets and require all overdue work to be recovered in the next period.',
'Enroll the member in technical training and assign the next complex task as an opportunity to practice.'
],'A performance change may reflect resource constraints, skill gaps, or personal difficulties. A factual one-to-one conversation helps diagnose the cause before choosing appropriate support and accountability measures.',[null,'Review may become a useful control, but adding supervision before understanding the cause may not resolve the delays.','Increasing targets before diagnosing the problem can make matters worse. Understand the actual barriers first.','There is no evidence that insufficient skill is the cause. Training and a greater challenge may be inappropriate.']);
put('a010','Training and capability','Foundation','A project will use an unfamiliar tool, and repeated operating errors have occurred during the pilot. The manager has a small training budget. What should be prioritized?',[
'Buy a comprehensive certification course and use the pass rate as evidence that the team is capable.',
'Identify the skill gaps relevant to delivery, provide targeted practice, and verify practical proficiency.',
'Introduce stricter reviews so a few skilled members correct every error before work is submitted.',
'Let each member choose learning materials independently and report study hours and reflections monthly.'
],'Training should target capabilities required for delivery and be validated through real tasks. Completing a course does not by itself demonstrate reliable performance.',['Certification may be useful, but its fit to the actual gap is unknown, and passing an exam may not demonstrate practical reliability.',null,'Review can catch defects but does not sustainably close the team’s skill gap and may create a bottleneck.','Self-directed learning can supplement training, but common capability targets and practical verification are still needed.']);
put('a011','Negotiating for resources','Advanced','A functional manager plans to reassign the only data specialist just before a critical acceptance event. The specialist does not report directly to the project manager. How should the project manager proceed?',[
'Move the acceptance date first to allow the functional manager enough time to arrange a handover.',
'Ask the specialist to prioritize the project, then explain the business impact to the functional manager afterward.',
'Use critical-path and business impacts to negotiate a shared-resource arrangement or an alternative with the functional manager.',
'Escalate the resource risk directly to the sponsor and ask the sponsor to order the specialist retained until acceptance.'
],'A matrix environment relies on negotiation. Connecting resource needs to project impacts and exploring workable alternatives is more effective than relying on authority the project manager does not have.',['Changing the date may require approval, and options that could avoid delay have not yet been negotiated.','Changing the specialist’s priorities first bypasses the resource manager. Agree on commitments before doing so.',null,'Escalation may be appropriate if negotiation fails, but first present the impact and attempt coordination at the current level.']);
put('a012','Shared vision','Foundation','Departments optimize different measures: development maximizes feature count, support emphasizes stability, and sales keeps adding requirements. Overall project value is declining. What should the leader promote first?',[
'Have each department improve its existing metrics and consolidate their results at the end of the month.',
'Ask the sponsor to select one department’s metric as the overriding goal and treat the others as reference information.',
'Hold more frequent cross-department status meetings to track each department’s performance measures.',
'Align the team and key stakeholders on common business objectives and shared measures of success.'
],'When local measures conflict, shared outcomes are needed so trade-offs serve overall value. Improving a single department’s metric in isolation may worsen the imbalance.',['Independent optimization is the existing problem; consolidating results does not resolve conflicting measures.','Choosing one local metric before discussing overall business outcomes may continue to sacrifice project value.','Visibility helps, but more tracking without shared objectives can reinforce local optimization.',null]);
put('a013','Facilitating decisions','Advanced','During a prioritization workshop, a vocal executive repeatedly interrupts user representatives. Time is nearly up, but no ranking has been agreed. What should the facilitator do?',[
'Establish turn-taking and shared prioritization criteria, then guide assessment using value and constraints.',
'Ask departments to submit written votes and rank requirements solely by the number of votes received.',
'Interview the executive and users separately afterward and create a compromise ranking as facilitator.',
'Give all remaining time to user representatives and complete the ranking based on their preferences.'
],'A facilitator protects equitable participation and helps the group reach usable decisions. Structured discussion surfaces information instead of letting the strongest voice determine priorities.',[null,'Voting may balance speaking opportunities, but without shared value criteria, vote totals may not represent business priorities.','Interviews can supplement information, but a private compromise skips shared understanding of criteria and trade-offs.','The user perspective matters, but replacing one dominant group with another still lacks balanced participation and agreed criteria.']);
put('a014','Resistance to change','Foundation','A new process will reduce manual data entry, but frontline employees fear losing their jobs and resist the pilot. How should the project leader respond?',[
'Provide comprehensive system training first, then discuss job impacts once operating proficiency is established.',
'Listen to specific concerns, clarify job impacts, and involve employees in pilot design and training.',
'Have senior leaders explain company benefits and require managers to achieve pilot participation targets.',
'Run the pilot only with supportive employees, then show the results to resistant employees after it succeeds.'
],'Resistance often comes from uncertainty and impacts on people’s interests. Transparent communication and meaningful participation support acceptance, without making unauthorized promises that no one will ever be affected.',['The stated concern is job security. Training alone does not address the central perceived impact.',null,'Promoting benefits and enforcing participation rates cannot replace two-way discussion and meaningful involvement.','A pilot with supporters can demonstrate value, but excluding affected groups misses their concerns and adoption barriers.']);
put('a015','Choosing a conflict approach','Advanced','An immediate safety hazard requires work to stop, but two supervisors are still arguing about whether construction can continue. What should the leader do now?',[
'Contact a safety expert for a written conclusion while allowing current work to continue until it arrives.',
'Facilitate a quick risk discussion between the supervisors and issue a joint stop-work order once they agree.',
'Immediately stop the affected work under safety procedures, then organize assessment and follow-up action.',
'Assign someone to monitor the hazard while requesting the sponsor’s approval to suspend the work.'
],'When a clear, urgent safety risk exists, authorized protective action comes first. Collaboration matters, but waiting for consensus must not delay the safety response.',['Expert advice matters, but a written opinion must not delay protection from an immediate hazard.','The need to stop is already established; waiting for consensus extends people’s exposure.',null,'Monitoring and approval requests do not replace immediate action already authorized by the safety procedure.']);
put('a016','Recognition and motivation','Foundation','Team members jointly solve a long-standing integration problem. The leader wants to reinforce collaboration rather than internal competition. What is the best form of recognition?',[
'Allocate rewards by the number of tasks each member completed to provide a consistent quantitative basis.',
'Give the full reward to the person who led the solution and let that person decide how to distribute it.',
'Give everyone the same reward and thank only the team as a whole, without identifying individual contributions.',
'Recognize the shared outcome and specifically acknowledge how different members contributed to collaboration.'
],'Recognition should reinforce the desired behavior. Making both the team outcome and specific individual contributions visible encourages collaboration without obscuring people’s contributions.',['Task counts may overlook difficulty, support work, and collaboration, so they do not align with the intended behavior.','Highlighting only the lead and transferring allocation responsibility may make other important contributions invisible.','Equal rewards can be appropriate, but general thanks alone miss the opportunity to reinforce specific collaborative behavior.',null]);
put('a017','Knowledge transfer','Foundation','A key engineer will leave in one month, and most system-maintenance knowledge is undocumented. What should the project manager prioritize?',[
'Create a risk-based handover plan combining paired work, documentation, and verification of the successor’s capability.',
'Require complete technical documentation before departure and let the successor study it after joining.',
'Prioritize recruiting an equally experienced replacement and rely on professional experience to cover system knowledge.',
'Record system demonstrations and store all recordings in the knowledge base for future staff.'
],'Knowledge transfer requires the recipient to perform independently, not merely to have access to materials. Risk-based prioritization and practical verification reduce single-person dependency within the limited time.',[null,'Documentation matters, but one-way delivery does not verify understanding or execution, and tacit knowledge may be missed.','General experience does not automatically replace system-specific context, and recruitment may miss the handover window.','Recordings can support transfer but do not verify that the recipient can perform critical tasks.']);
put('a018','Communication effectiveness','Foundation','A project manager sends detailed weekly reports, but the business owner still cannot tell whether action is needed. What is the best improvement?',[
'Add more detailed schedule and cost data so the business owner can identify trends independently.',
'Confirm the business owner’s decision needs and emphasize risks, impacts, and decisions awaiting action.',
'Hold weekly status meetings and explain every data point in the report section by section.',
'Adopt the company’s standard executive report template to ensure sufficiently complete communication.'
],'Communication succeeds when the recipient can understand information and act on it. Content and format must match information needs; page count is not a measure of effectiveness.',['More detail may not support action and could continue to obscure the decisions required.',null,'Explaining each section may help comprehension, but first establish which information supports the recipient’s decisions.','Templates are useful, but a standard format cannot be assumed to meet this business owner’s specific needs.']);
put('a019','Emotional intelligence','Foundation','A customer becomes upset about a delay and criticizes the project team during a meeting. The manager has preliminary delay data. What is the best response?',[
'Immediately present responsibility assignments and delay data to establish what is not the team’s fault.',
'Offer the most likely recovery date first, then ask the team to assess whether it is feasible.',
'Remain calm, acknowledge the customer’s impact, clarify concerns, and use facts to discuss recovery options.',
'Suspend the meeting so both sides can calm down and reconnect only after the internal root-cause analysis is complete.'
],'Managing one’s own reaction and acknowledging the other party’s concerns creates space for a factual discussion. Recognizing the impact does not mean accepting all blame or promising impossible dates.',['Facts matter, but beginning with blame allocation may escalate emotions and obscure the customer’s actual concern.','A recovery date needs evidence; offering one before assessment may be interpreted as a commitment.',null,'A short break may sometimes help, but the manager can listen and communicate known facts now rather than wait for a complete analysis.']);
put('a020','Cross-team dependencies','Advanced','Two agile teams share an interface. Each achieves its iteration goals, but integration conflicts repeatedly appear at release time. What is the most effective improvement?',[
'Fully align the teams’ iteration calendars and integrate all work together on the final day of each iteration.',
'Create a separate integration team to own interface problems while both original teams retain their delivery patterns.',
'Expand interface documentation and hold a joint review immediately before the formal release.',
'Visualize dependencies and interface agreements jointly, and validate cross-team integration earlier and continuously.'
],'Local completion is not the same as an integrated, deliverable result. Explicit dependency management and shorter integration feedback cycles expose cross-team problems earlier.',['Aligned calendars can help coordination, but integration feedback still arrives at the end of the iteration.','A specialist team may help, but it can push issues downstream rather than promote joint upstream dependency management.','Documentation and reviews help, but validation just before release may discover incompatibility too late.',null]);
put('a021','Clarifying roles','Foundation','The product owner and project manager give the team conflicting priority instructions. The team does not know whose direction to follow. What should happen first?',[
'Clarify decision responsibilities, establish one prioritization channel and an escalation mechanism, and communicate them to the team.',
'Have the two leaders coordinate privately each day, then send their agreed instructions to the team separately.',
'Ask the team to log conflicting instructions and let the project manager make the final decision at daily meetings.',
'Rank instructions by the leaders’ organizational seniority and publish this hierarchy to all team members.'
],'The conflict comes from unclear responsibilities and decision interfaces. Clarifying who decides what reduces duplicate direction and contradictory work requests.',[null,'Private coordination can relieve tension, but clear responsibilities and a single prioritization channel are still missing.','This assumes the project manager has all prioritization authority without clarifying the product owner’s responsibilities.','Position in the hierarchy does not replace the decision rights assigned to specific roles and product governance.']);
put('a022','Team development','Foundation','Soon after kickoff, a new team begins arguing about working methods and role boundaries. How should the manager view and address this?',[
'Immediately add detailed procedures requiring everyone to follow identical steps to reduce disagreement.',
'Treat disagreements as a sign of adjustment, clarify roles and norms, and facilitate constructive discussion.',
'Agree on each person’s duties individually and defer group discussion until everyone is familiar with the work.',
'Concentrate on delivery first and discuss collaboration differences after the next milestone.'
],'Disagreement after team formation does not necessarily mean failure. The leader should help clarify roles and collaboration methods so conflict becomes an opportunity to establish shared norms.',['Procedures can support collaboration, but prescribing every method too early may suppress the team’s necessary adjustment process.',null,'Individual clarification helps, but interfaces and common working methods still need collective alignment.','If disagreement is already affecting collaboration, delaying intervention may entrench it and harm the milestone.']);
put('a023','Coaching and mentoring','Advanced','An experienced developer wants to improve retrospective facilitation. The developer understands the basics but does not know where to improve. How should the leader help?',[
'Provide a standard retrospective agenda and require its use three times before the developer identifies lessons independently.',
'Have the developer observe an expert and resume independent facilitation only after mastering all techniques.',
'Observe an actual session, give specific feedback, and use questions to help the developer choose practice goals.',
'Explain the leader’s preferred facilitation techniques and have the developer follow them step by step next time.'
],'For someone with a foundation, observation-based coaching supports self-awareness and continued improvement. Taking over or offering only general advice is less effective at developing independent capability.',['An agenda is useful, but it does not identify the person’s current gaps or provide timely, targeted feedback.','Observation helps, but practice should not be delayed indefinitely. Direct feedback is more targeted for someone who already knows the basics.',null,'Sharing techniques is mentoring, but observing actual behavior and reflecting on it better identifies this individual’s improvement opportunities.']);
put('a024','Inclusive communication','Foundation','During remote training, a team member with a hearing impairment cannot fully understand an uncaptioned presentation. What should the project leader do?',[
'Arrange a separate question-and-answer session afterward, with a colleague explaining the main training points.',
'Send the full handout and substitute reading for the member’s participation in the presentation and discussion.',
'Ask the instructor to slow down and add more spoken explanation while retaining the existing platform.',
'Confirm an effective approach with the member and provide captions, written materials, and accessible interaction channels.'
],'Effective collaboration requires equitable access to information. Provide accessible formats based on actual needs rather than assuming one communication medium works for everyone.',['Compensating afterward does not fully replace equal participation in live training. First identify and remove access barriers.','Handouts help, but they need not replace opportunities to participate in demonstrations, feedback, and discussion.','Slower or additional speech may not meet the member’s needs. Confirm an accessible approach with them.',null]);
put('a025','Organizational influence','Advanced','A project needs data from sales and finance, but the project manager cannot assign work to either department. What is the best approach?',[
'Explain how the data supports shared business goals and negotiate accountable contacts and delivery commitments.',
'Put submission dates into the project plan for both departments and copy the sponsor on tracking messages.',
'Ask the sponsor to issue a formal instruction for every data request to establish the project’s authority.',
'Ask familiar employees to extract the data informally and notify their managers after the work is completed.'
],'Without formal authority, influence comes through shared objectives, trusted relationships, and clear agreements. Collaboration requires commitments and interfaces, not unilateral orders.',[null,'A date in a plan does not create a departmental resource commitment. Negotiate with those who have authority first.','Senior support can be used when necessary, but relying on escalation for every request skips negotiation at the current level.','Personal relationships can help, but a lack of departmental commitment may create priority and data-accountability issues.']);
put('a026','Sustainable pace','Foundation','A team has worked overtime for six weeks to meet a release. Defect rates and intentions to leave are both rising. What should the leader do?',[
'Arrange a block of recovery leave, then continue with the original release scope and date.',
'Reassess capacity and priorities with the team and product owner, reduce work in progress, and establish a sustainable pace.',
'Offer rewards for voluntary overtime and introduce stricter testing to control defects.',
'Immediately add people to take all overdue tasks while requiring the original team to maintain its current speed.'
],'Sustained overload reduces quality and effective output. Rebalance demand against capacity and address flow problems rather than treating overtime as normal capacity.',['Recovery time helps, but the same overload is likely to recur unless demand and capacity are rebalanced.',null,'Incentives and testing do not remove sustained overload; fatigue can continue harming quality and retention.','New people require assessment and onboarding. Immediate capacity gains cannot be assumed as a reason to leave workload unchanged.']);
put('a027','Feedback culture','Foundation','A tester is mocked by the development lead after raising a design risk. Other members begin withholding risks. What should the manager do?',[
'Create an anonymous risk channel and have the project manager filter concerns before they are made public.',
'Have development and testing leads verify the technical evidence before discussing how they interacted.',
'Promptly stop the disrespectful behavior, explain its impact, and reinforce shared norms for safe feedback.',
'Move risk discussions to written reviews to reduce direct verbal conflict during meetings.'
],'The negative interaction is already damaging information flow. Address the specific behavior, protect channels for raising concerns, and demonstrate support for constructive feedback.',['An anonymous channel can provide extra protection but does not directly address ridicule or unsafe public interaction.','Technical evidence deserves verification, but harmful behavior should not be left unaddressed until the technical conclusion is known.',null,'Changing the medium may reduce live conflict without resolving attitudes toward feedback or shared behavioral norms.']);
put('a028','Stakeholder analysis','Advanced','An influential department head initially supported a project but now opposes it because it is consuming departmental resources. How should the project manager adjust?',[
'Ask the sponsor to restate the project’s priority and require the department head to honor the original support commitment.',
'Send the department head more benefits reports to demonstrate the long-term organizational value of the resource investment.',
'Reclassify the department head as an opponent and involve them only when approval of major matters is required.',
'Reassess the department head’s interests and engagement, then develop negotiation actions addressing the resource impact.'
],'Stakeholder attitudes change with circumstances. Adapt engagement strategy to the new impact on interests rather than relying on the initial assessment.',['Sponsor support may help, but first understand the resource impact rather than simply demanding the earlier attitude.','Benefits information does not replace targeted negotiation about the immediate resource conflict.','Labeling the stakeholder and reducing participation may worsen resistance while leaving a potentially solvable issue unaddressed.',null]);
put('a029','Individual recognition preferences','Foundation','One member dislikes being praised by name in all-hands meetings, while another values public recognition. How should the leader design recognition?',[
'Understand individual preferences and use suitable forms of recognition while applying consistent contribution criteria.',
'Use private written praise for everyone so all members receive exactly the same form of recognition.',
'Have the team vote for one recognition method and require the minority to follow the majority preference.',
'Convert all recognition into quantitative bonuses to avoid judging individual communication and motivation preferences.'
],'Fairness does not require identical forms of recognition. Contribution criteria should be consistent, while the way appreciation is expressed can fit individual preferences.',[null,'A uniform approach may avoid some discomfort but may not fit everyone. Consistent contribution standards are the key to fairness.','The majority’s preference need not suit every individual; recognition can vary within a fair framework.','Bonuses may not replace the recognition experience people value, nor eliminate the need to understand their preferences.']);
put('a030','Team decision-making','Advanced','The team must choose between two reversible interface designs. Data is limited, discussion has stalled, and the product owner permits a small experiment. What is the best approach?',[
'Implement the cheaper design and consider changing it only if customers complain.',
'Define user-focused success measures, run a short experiment, and select the design based on the results.',
'Have a design expert assess both options against industry best practices and adopt the recommendation.',
'Conduct an anonymous team vote, complete the selected design, and then schedule usability testing.'
],'For a reversible, testable choice, a small experiment obtains evidence at limited cost. Continued debate or a decision based only on authority does not close the data gap.',['Cost matters, but an experiment is permitted and user evidence is missing. Waiting for complaints delays learning.',null,'Expert experience can inform a hypothesis, but direct user testing is stronger evidence for a choice that can be tested.','An internal vote does not provide missing user evidence and defers validation until after the decision and development.']);
put('a031','Escalating conflict','Advanced','Two departments have repeatedly negotiated resource allocation under their agreed process without reaching agreement. Delay threatens an organization-wide regulatory deadline. What should the project manager do next?',[
'Arrange another negotiation and offer a compromise allocation as the starting point.',
'Resequence project activities to avoid the conflict as far as possible before considering escalation.',
'Follow governance procedures to present facts, deadline impacts, and alternatives to the authority able to decide.',
'Refer the dispute to human resources to allocate people according to their departmental reporting lines.'
],'The parties have already tried to resolve the matter locally, and a major organizational constraint is involved. Escalation is appropriate when it supplies decision-relevant information rather than merely shifting responsibility.',['Repeated negotiation has failed and the regulatory deadline is pressing. Continuing at a level without decision rights can waste time.','Resequencing can be an alternative, but the organization-level deadline risk now needs a timely authorized decision.',null,'Business governance must decide the resource trade-off. Employment reporting lines do not determine project-priority authority.']);
put('a032','Team participation in estimates','Foundation','A business executive asks the project leader alone to provide precise durations for every backlog item. The team has not yet clarified the requirements. How should the leader respond?',[
'Make a uniform commitment based on past projects, then have the team break down the tasks.',
'Ask the most senior architect to estimate every item to keep technical judgment and estimation consistent.',
'Use the requested business date as the maximum estimate and require the team to decompose work to fit it.',
'Involve the people doing the work in clarifying requirements and estimating, and explain assumptions and uncertainty.'
],'The people performing the work know its details. Their involvement improves estimates and commitment. Early estimates should express uncertainty rather than pretend to be precise guarantees.',['Historical data helps, but committing before clarification locks in unverified assumptions.','Senior expertise matters, but it does not replace information and understanding from the actual implementers.','A target date is a constraint; it should not be presented as an effort-based estimate.',null]);
put('a033','Incident communication','Advanced','A go-live incident is affecting customers and engineers are investigating. Several executives keep messaging developers directly for updates, repeatedly interrupting recovery. What should the leader do?',[
'Create one incident communication channel, designate an information coordinator, and issue updates at agreed intervals.',
'Have the development lead answer every executive inquiry to provide decision-makers with immediate technical information.',
'Create a shared technical log for executives to read independently and report again only after recovery.',
'Collect all executive questions into hourly meetings and require every developer to attend the updates.'
],'Incident response must protect engineers’ focus while meeting stakeholder information needs. Clear communication ownership and cadence reduce repeated interruptions.',[null,'Direct contact is timely but repeatedly interrupts a key person working on recovery.','A log supports transparency, but without proactive updates and an explanation channel, repeated inquiries may continue.','Grouping questions reduces scattered interruptions, but requiring every developer at recurring meetings still consumes unnecessary recovery capacity.']);
put('a034','Scope baseline','Foundation','A customer requests an additional report. The team estimates two days of work, but the project already has an approved scope baseline. How should the project manager respond?',[
'Use schedule reserve and the existing budget to build the report, then mention the scope change in the weekly report.',
'Log the change request, assess its integrated scope, cost, and other impacts, and decide through the change-control process.',
'Confirm the report’s acceptance criteria with the customer and let the team decide whether to include it now.',
'Put the report into the next release as an improvement that does not affect the current baseline.'
],'Even a small change can affect testing, cost, and other commitments. When a baseline exists, follow the applicable control process, obtain authorization, and then update plans and implement.',['Available budget and reserve do not automatically authorize a scope change; established controls still apply.',null,'Clarifying requirements helps, but allowing the team to include the work on its own bypasses baseline-change authority.','Deferring implementation does not remove the need for assessment and authorization; a later release also commits scope and resources.']);
put('a035','Requirements traceability','Foundation','During acceptance, a contractual requirement is found to be missing. The team believes it was lost through several requirement changes. Which practice best prevents a recurrence?',[
'Send complete minutes after every requirement change and ask attendees to confirm that nothing was missed.',
'Reread the contract at the end of each milestone and have the project manager check completed work.',
'Maintain links from requirements to deliverables, tests, and acceptance criteria, updating them when changes occur.',
'Maintain a detailed priority list so the highest-priority requirements enter development first.'
],'Traceability shows whether each requirement has been designed, implemented, and verified, and exposes downstream change impacts. A requirements list alone does not demonstrate coverage.',['Confirmed minutes help but do not systematically link requirements to implementation and verification throughout later stages.','Milestone checks can detect issues, but ongoing traceability reveals gaps and change impacts earlier.',null,'Prioritization is not coverage tracking; lower-priority contractual requirements must not disappear either.']);
put('a036','Work breakdown structure','Foundation','The team has listed activities such as weekly meetings and writing emails but cannot tell whether the final deliverables are complete. What is the best improvement?',[
'Break every known activity into daily tasks and sequence them into a project network diagram.',
'Allocate the activity list by department and require each functional manager to add missing tasks.',
'Put the major contract milestones into the plan and add activities progressively during execution.',
'Decompose scope around final deliverables into manageable work packages and check completeness.'
],'A WBS represents the complete scope to be delivered; activities describe how the work is performed. Deliverable-based decomposition helps reveal omissions and clarify responsibility.',['Activity detail and sequencing support scheduling but cannot replace identifying the complete delivery scope first.','Departmental input is useful, but it may miss cross-functional deliverables unless completeness is checked against overall outcomes.','Milestones are checkpoints, not necessarily a complete scope definition. Waiting until execution can miss necessary work.',null]);
put('a037','Critical path','Advanced','Two parallel paths must both finish before project completion: A→B takes 12 days and C→D takes 9 days. With all other conditions unchanged, what happens if activity C is shortened by two days?',[
'The project still takes 12 days because the longer A→B path is unchanged.',
'The project takes 10 days because two days saved on either path bring completion forward.',
'The project takes 7 days because the shortened C→D path becomes the controlling path.',
'The project still takes 12 days because both paths now have two days of float available to share.'
],'The longest dependency path determines the earliest project completion. Shortening a noncritical path increases its float without changing the current critical path’s length.',[null,'Overall duration can decrease only when the path that constrains completion is shortened.','The longest path controls completion, not the shortest.','The duration is correct but the explanation is not: A→B remains critical and does not gain two days of float.']);
put('a038','Schedule compression','Advanced','A critical-path activity takes 10 days. Adding experienced people can reduce it to 7 days at higher cost. Its predecessor and successor cannot overlap with it. Which compression technique is being used?',[
'Fast tracking: increasing work overlap to shorten the overall project schedule.',
'Crashing: adding resources or cost to reduce the duration of a critical activity.',
'Resource smoothing: using activity float to adjust the timing of existing resources.',
'Re-estimating: revising the original duration estimate based on new information.'
],'Crashing shortens duration by adding resources or similar measures and usually increases cost. Fast tracking overlaps activities that were sequential; the scenario explicitly rules out overlap.',['Fast tracking requires overlapping activities, whereas this scenario uses additional resources.',null,'The team is not using float to smooth workload; it is adding people and incurring additional cost.','The reduction comes from a specific resource investment, not simply a revised estimate.']);
put('a039','Earned value analysis','Advanced','A project has EV of 800,000, AC of 1,000,000, and PV of 900,000. Which assessment is correct?',[
'Over budget and ahead of schedule: CPI = 0.80 and SPI ≈ 1.13.',
'Under budget and behind schedule: CPI = 1.25 and SPI ≈ 0.89.',
'Over budget and behind schedule: CPI = 0.80 and SPI ≈ 0.89.',
'Over budget and behind schedule: CPI ≈ 0.89 and SPI = 0.80.'
],'CPI = EV/AC = 800,000/1,000,000, and SPI = EV/PV = 800,000/900,000. Both are below 1: spending exceeds earned value, and completed work is below the planned value.',['CPI is correct, but SPI is EV/PV, not PV/EV.','SPI is correct, but CPI is EV/AC. Reversing the ratio reverses the cost interpretation.',null,'The overall direction is right, but the denominators are swapped: cost is compared with AC and schedule with PV.']);
put('a040','Estimate at completion','Advanced','The project BAC is 2,000,000 and the current CPI is 0.80. If current cost efficiency is expected to continue, what is the estimated EAC?',[
'1,600,000.','2,000,000.','2,400,000.','2,500,000.'
],'If current cost efficiency continues, EAC = BAC/CPI = 2,000,000/0.80 = 2,500,000. Establish the forecasting assumption first; other scenarios may call for different formulas.',['Multiplying BAC by CPI understates the completion cost. Divide by CPI when current efficiency is expected to continue.','This assumes completion within the original budget and ignores the continuing efficiency loss.','Adding 20% of the budget is not equivalent to the cost required at an efficiency of 0.80.',null]);
put('a041','Cost variance','Foundation','As of the reporting date, EV is 500,000 and AC is 650,000. What is cost variance (CV), and what does it mean?',[
'−150,000: actual spending exceeds the earned value of completed work, indicating a current cost overrun.',
'+150,000: actual spending is higher than the budgeted value of the work completed.',
'−150,000: the final project cost will definitely exceed the budget by 150,000.',
'+150,000: completed work has achieved better cost efficiency than planned.'
],'CV = EV − AC = 500,000 − 650,000 = −150,000. A negative value indicates an overrun for work completed to date; it does not automatically equal the final overrun.',[null,'The interpretation recognizes overspending, but the sign is reversed. CV uses EV − AC.','Current variance is not a guarantee of the final overrun; future performance and remaining work affect the outcome.','Actual cost exceeds earned value, so neither positive CV nor improved cost efficiency is supported.']);
put('a042','Risks and issues','Foundation','A supply interruption recorded in the risk register has occurred, preventing materials from arriving. How should the project manager handle records and action?',[
'Change the risk probability to 100% and wait for the next risk review to decide on action.',
'Track the actual impact as an issue, activate the applicable response, and update related risk information.',
'Immediately spend the reserved funds on replacement materials and check response-plan authority afterward.',
'Move the event to the issue log, close the original risk, and stop monitoring any related residual risk.'
],'A risk is an uncertain event. Once it occurs, the actual obstacle needs issue management and accountable action. The risk record can retain its history and reflect residual risks.',['The realized impact already needs issue management. Merely changing probability and waiting for a routine review is insufficient.',null,'Action should be timely, but purchasing and use of funds must follow the applicable plan and authority.','Issue tracking is appropriate, but residual or secondary risks arising from the event and response may still need management.']);
put('a043','Risk responses','Foundation','A high-risk technology is not yet mature enough for a project. The team replaces it with a proven solution that meets the business need, removing the source of the threat from the plan. Which response strategy is this?',[
'Mitigate.','Transfer.','Avoid.','Accept.'
],'Avoidance changes the plan to eliminate a threat or protect objectives from its impact. Here the immature technology is removed, rather than merely reducing its probability.',['Mitigation reduces probability or impact; this choice removes the source of the threat.','Responsibility is not being transferred to a third party through a contract or similar arrangement.',null,'The team actively changes the plan rather than retaining the original solution and bearing the risk.']);
put('a044','Risk ownership','Foundation','Several high risks list “the team” as owner, but nobody knows who should act when a trigger occurs. What most needs improvement?',[
'Have the project manager track every risk centrally and assign response tasks after an event occurs.',
'Assign risks to functional departments and ask each department to report its actions at month end.',
'Increase risk-review frequency so attendees can jointly decide each time which items require action.',
'Assign a specific owner, defined triggers, and executable response arrangements for each risk.'
],'Collective attention does not replace clear accountability. Risk owners drive monitoring and response; triggers support timely action, and actionable arrangements turn strategy into execution.',['Central tracking does not establish specialist response ownership, and allocating tasks after a trigger may delay action.','Departmental assignment improves visibility somewhat but can still lack an individual owner and timely triggering mechanism.','Frequent reviews support management but do not replace item-level ownership and planned responses.',null]);
put('a045','Managing opportunities','Advanced','A supplier offers a proven assembly method that could lower costs. Realizing the benefit requires a joint pilot and shared gains, so the team partners with the supplier. Which opportunity response best fits?',[
'Share.','Exploit.','Enhance.','Accept.'
],'Sharing an opportunity involves partnering with a party better positioned to realize it and sharing the associated effort and benefit. The joint pilot and shared gains match that approach.',[null,'Exploitation focuses on ensuring an opportunity occurs; the distinguishing feature here is joint realization and shared benefit.','Enhancement increases probability or impact, whereas the partnership and shared gains specifically indicate sharing.','The team is actively arranging a partnership and pilot rather than simply taking advantage of an opportunity if it arises.']);
put('a046','Preventing quality defects','Foundation','Final inspection repeatedly finds the same assembly error, causing expensive rework. What is the most valuable next step?',[
'Increase final-inspection sampling to detect more of these assembly errors before shipment.',
'Analyze the recurring error’s process root causes and improve work design or introduce appropriate mistake-proofing.',
'Retrain every assembler and require signed confirmation that operating procedures will be followed.',
'Create a dedicated rework team to reduce the delay between discovering and correcting errors.'
],'Final inspection detects defects, but prevention acts on the process that creates them. Root-cause analysis and mistake-proofing reduce recurrence rather than simply filtering defects later.',['More inspection can reduce escaped defects but does not prevent the errors from being produced.',null,'Training may be part of the solution, but assuming an operator problem before analysis may overlook process or design causes.','Faster rework improves correction, not prevention of recurring defects.']);
put('a047','Acceptance and quality control','Advanced','The team has passed internal testing and met all known technical measures. The customer has not yet confirmed that the deliverable meets business acceptance criteria. How should this status be understood?',[
'Technical compliance is sufficient to close the project now and obtain the customer’s signature afterward.',
'Send the test results and assume acceptance if the customer does not object within the normal reporting cycle.',
'Internal technical quality has been verified, but formal acceptance by the customer or authorized party is still required.',
'Ask the customer to repeat every internal test and use the passing results instead of business acceptance.'
],'Technical conformance and formal acceptance serve different purposes. Passing internal tests does not automatically replace acceptance against the criteria agreed with the customer.',['Formal acceptance is still outstanding; it should not be treated as a formality to complete after closure.','Deemed acceptance would require an agreed contractual mechanism, which the scenario does not establish.',null,'Test evidence can support acceptance, but business acceptance criteria cannot simply be replaced by repeating technical tests.']);
put('a048','Procurement contracts','Advanced','A fixed-price contract has a well-defined scope. The supplier requests an unconditional price increase because its own estimate was wrong; the scope has not changed. What should the procurement lead do first?',[
'Request evidence of the additional cost and submit a budget increase for the verified difference.',
'Refuse any discussion on fixed-price grounds and wait for the supplier to deliver to the original plan.',
'Agree to split the cost difference equally to preserve the relationship and continuity of delivery.',
'Check the contract terms and actual circumstances, then handle the price request under those terms.'
],'Contract type influences cost-risk allocation, but rights and obligations depend on the actual terms and facts. Without a scope change, a supplier’s estimating error generally does not automatically authorize a price increase.',['Cost evidence is useful, but requesting the difference before interpreting the contract assumes the buyer must bear it.','Contract type offers direction, but specific terms and delivery risks still need examination rather than stopping engagement.','A compromise is not automatically correct. Cost responsibility must first be grounded in contractual obligations and overall impact.',null]);
put('a049','Procurement selection','Foundation','The team is buying equipment with demanding reliability requirements. The lowest bidder has weak maintenance capability and high downtime risk. Which selection method is most appropriate?',[
'Evaluate against predefined criteria covering technical suitability, life-cycle cost, and ongoing service capability.',
'Select the lowest initial price and negotiate an extended warranty to reduce future downtime impacts.',
'Choose the supplier with the strongest maintenance network and address price during contract negotiations.',
'Request additional reliability commitments from every supplier and rank them only by the promised availability.'
],'Procurement value extends beyond purchase price. Reliability-sensitive equipment requires maintenance, downtime, and ongoing support to be considered in the predefined evaluation criteria.',[null,'A warranty may transfer some costs but cannot fully compensate for downtime and weak service capability; evaluate the overall offer first.','Maintenance matters, but choosing on one strength overlooks the agreed combination of technical and cost criteria.','Commitments need verification and contractual support; promised availability does not replace assessment of actual capability and life-cycle cost.']);
put('a050','Contract changes','Advanced','A customer asks an outsourced developer directly for an extra feature, and the developer is about to start. What should the project manager do?',[
'Have the contractor estimate the work and agree a price with the customer, then start as soon as both agree.',
'Confirm the request and obtain assessment and authorization through the agreed contract and project-change interfaces before implementation.',
'Allow the supplier to start parts that do not affect the current schedule and settle additional costs later.',
'Add the request to the next iteration backlog and instruct the supplier to implement it once the product owner ranks it.'
],'An external request may not come from someone authorized to change the contract. Clear change interfaces prevent disputes over cost, scope, and responsibility.',['A technical estimate helps, but the customer contact and developer may not have contractual change authority.',null,'Even without a current schedule impact, unauthorized extra work can create scope and payment disputes.','Product prioritization does not necessarily constitute contract authorization; outsourced scope changes still need the applicable commercial process.']);
put('a051','Integration management','Foundation','A schedule improvement could save one week but increase cost and reduce testing time. How should the project manager decide?',[
'Approve it if the business benefit of the saved week exceeds the extra labor cost.',
'Use the test lead’s confirmation that remaining tests can be completed as the approval basis.',
'Assess schedule, cost, and quality risks against business objectives, then seek approval at the appropriate authority level.',
'Obtain sponsor approval for the extra cost and let the team adjust testing during implementation.'
],'Project decisions require integration across constraints. A local schedule gain may become quality risk and operating cost; one measure should not be optimized in isolation.',['Benefits and labor cost matter, but this comparison omits quality and risk costs created by reduced testing.','Testing confirmation addresses only one dimension; cost, risk, and other commitments still need integration.',null,'Approval of expenditure does not show that other impacts were assessed. Deferring quality arrangements may be too late.']);
put('a052','Backlog prioritization','Foundation','A product owner considers ten features. One would quickly test the largest business assumption, while another is easiest to build. What should primarily guide priority?',[
'Choose the smallest development effort first to maximize the number of visible outputs in this iteration.',
'Deliver the feature promised earliest to stakeholders first and keep the order stable to maintain trust.',
'Rank solely by expected revenue divided by development cost and choose the highest financial ratio.',
'Consider value, risk, dependencies, and learning value in relation to business objectives.'
],'Ease of development is only one cost factor. Work that tests a critical assumption may deliver substantial learning value, so prioritization must connect to product goals and risk.',['Small batches help, but prioritizing only ease can delay testing the most important business assumption.','Commitments matter, but emerging evidence can require new value and risk trade-offs.','A simple financial ratio can omit learning value, dependencies, and risk reduction.',null]);
put('a053','Iteration goal','Advanced','During an iteration, a business stakeholder proposes a new idea. It is not urgent and does not affect the current iteration goal. The team is nearly at capacity. What is the best response?',[
'Have the product owner assess and prioritize the request while protecting the current iteration goal and focus.',
'Replace an iteration item with the new idea if both have the same estimated effort.',
'Have the team create a small prototype before the product owner decides whether to include the idea.',
'Log the idea and immediately convene the business stakeholder to decide that day whether to change the goal.'
],'Agility allows adaptation, but not every request requires an immediate interruption. A nonurgent change can enter prioritization while the current goal and focus are maintained.',[null,'Equal effort does not imply equal contribution to the goal or dependencies. A nonurgent request should not be swapped in without prioritization.','A prototype still consumes scarce capacity; establish priority and the value of interruption first.','Timely logging is appropriate, but immediately mobilizing for a change that is neither urgent nor goal-relevant can create unnecessary disruption.']);
put('a054','Definition of Done','Foundation','The team counts “coding complete” as done, but substantial testing and documentation remain after every review. How should it improve?',[
'Track coding, testing, and documentation separately while continuing to count coding completion as iteration output.',
'Agree on a Definition of Done that includes testing and delivery conditions, and report progress against it.',
'Hold a dedicated test phase every three iterations to finish outstanding verification and documentation.',
'Ask the product owner to accept each coded item and leave remaining work to the maintenance team.'
],'A Definition of Done provides shared standards for increment quality and usability. Counting only coding completion hides remaining work and undermines transparency and forecasts.',['Separate tracking helps, but treating work that has not met shared usable-quality criteria as done still overstates output.',null,'Deferring necessary completion work accumulates hidden unfinished work rather than ensuring reliable quality in each increment.','Product acceptance does not replace required quality and delivery conditions, and unfinished work should not automatically be passed to maintenance.']);
put('a055','Kanban and work in progress','Advanced','New items keep entering development on a Kanban board while testing has a large backlog. The team wants to reduce average delivery time. What should it do first?',[
'Split testing into smaller batches while allowing developers to start unlimited new work.',
'Increase development-completion targets so testing has more items to choose from and can stay busy.',
'Limit upstream work in progress and help the testing bottleneck so started work flows through to completion.',
'Raise the test team’s individual performance targets and measure improvement by items closed.'
],'The bottleneck constrains system throughput. Starting more work increases queues; controlling WIP and supporting the bottleneck shortens the time from start to completion.',['Smaller batches can help, but unlimited upstream starts continue adding to the testing queue.','Testing is already the constraint. More input only increases WIP and waiting.',null,'Pressure on local performance is not a substitute for cross-functional help and system-wide flow improvement.']);
put('a056','Relative estimation','Foundation','Team A normally completes 40 points and Team B completes 25. An executive concludes that Team A is 60% more productive. How should the project leader explain the comparison?',[
'The ranking is useful if supplemented with the teams’ average years of experience.',
'The point totals will accurately measure productivity differences once iteration lengths are equal.',
'Divide points by team size and use points per person as the main basis for budget and resource decisions.',
'Points are a team-relative scale and cannot directly compare productivity across different teams.'
],'Story points express relative complexity and effort within a team. Differences in scale, skill, and work type make simple cross-team comparisons misleading.',['Adding experience measures does not make the underlying point scales comparable.','Equal iteration lengths do not standardize estimation scales, work types, or Definitions of Done.','A per-person calculation does not turn different relative scales into objectively comparable output.',null]);
put('a057','Interpreting a burndown chart','Advanced','An iteration burndown chart has been flat for several days. The team says work is in progress but no item yet meets the Definition of Done. What is the most reasonable response?',[
'Examine item slicing, impediments, and remaining work, interpreting the chart with information from the team.',
'Increase individual progress-reporting frequency and use hours worked to explain the flat line.',
'Convert in-progress items’ completion percentages into burndown to show the effort being invested.',
'Extend the iteration until the line reaches zero so the original commitment is fully met.'
],'A burndown chart reflects remaining work, not effort directly. A flat line can result from large items, impediments, or update issues, so it needs interpretation with team information.',[null,'Hours worked do not demonstrate remaining work or value delivered; inspect flow and completion conditions.','Without a reliable remaining-work convention, subjective completion percentages can disguise unfinished work.','The chart alone does not justify an extension; extending the iteration avoids investigating the cause and adapting transparently.']);
put('a058','Hybrid delivery','Advanced','A medical-device project requires strict hardware approvals, while its software interface requirements will evolve through user feedback. Which approach is most suitable?',[
'Use a predictive baseline for the whole project and defer all new software feedback until project completion.',
'Use controlled planning and approvals for hardware, iterative feedback for software, and coordinate their interfaces.',
'Run the entire project as software iterations, reconsidering hardware procurement and approvals every iteration.',
'Manage hardware milestones and software iterations separately and integrate them only at final acceptance.'
],'Delivery approaches should reflect uncertainty and constraints in different types of work. A hybrid approach can combine controlled approval with rapid learning, provided integration, dependencies, and acceptance are coordinated.',['This respects hardware constraints but postpones the learning and validation needed for software.',null,'Hardware lead times and approval constraints may not suit arbitrary reconsideration each iteration.','Different approaches can coexist, but waiting until both finish exposes interfaces and dependencies too late.']);
put('a059','Lessons learned','Foundation','Midway through a project, the team validates an effective defect-prevention technique that could immediately help other teams. When should it be recorded and shared?',[
'Wait until project closure to confirm long-term effectiveness before entering it in the organizational knowledge base.',
'Discuss it in the team retrospective and share it only when another team asks about a similar issue.',
'Record and share it promptly after validation, refining its applicability as more experience is gained.',
'Immediately make it a mandatory company-wide standard without further validation in other contexts.'
],'Lessons learned belong throughout the project life cycle. Timely use creates value; waiting for closure misses opportunities to improve this project and others.',['Long-term observation can continue without delaying a validated lesson that is useful now.','Internal reflection helps, but waiting passively misses opportunities to prevent defects in other teams.',null,'Sharing knowledge does not justify universal mandatory adoption; context and governance authority still matter.']);
put('a060','Closure and handover','Foundation','The customer has accepted the deliverable, but the support team lacks maintenance instructions and the supplier’s final settlement is incomplete. Can formal project closure be considered complete?',[
'Close after acceptance and let the former team finish maintenance documents and settlement when time allows.',
'Release all project resources first and let operations contact the supplier to finish the remaining work.',
'Keep the project running indefinitely until operations needs no assistance of any kind from the project team.',
'Complete operational knowledge transfer, contract settlement, and required archiving, then close under organizational rules.'
],'Acceptance is a major milestone but not the whole closure process. Operational transition and commercial obligations must be addressed before the project is closed under organizational rules.',['Customer acceptance does not remove outstanding closure obligations or justify leaving them as informal spare-time work.','Resource release must be coordinated with handover responsibilities and contractual arrangements, not precede them.','Closure needs clear handover and responsibility boundaries, not a guarantee that no future support will ever be needed.',null]);
put('a061','Configuration management','Foundation','The test team is using last week’s interface document, while developers have implemented an updated version. This causes many false defect reports. What control is most needed?',[
'Establish controlled versions and a change distribution process so everyone works from the same interface definition.','Hold a daily interface meeting where developers explain the latest changes to testers.','Require testers to request the latest document from the development lead before every test.','Freeze the current interface document until release and treat all further changes as requirements for the next version.'
],'Configuration management identifies valid versions and controls changes to them. Inconsistent versions cause incorrect conclusions; simply increasing the amount of testing will not resolve the cause.',[
null,'Meetings can help, but without controlled versions and records, coordination still depends on memory and verbal communication.','Requesting files manually creates dependencies without establishing consistent version identification, change control, or distribution.','A freeze may be appropriate in some circumstances, but necessary changes should not be excluded merely to solve a version distribution problem.'
]);
put('a062','Resource leveling','Advanced','The same specialist is scheduled for two activities on the same day, both of which require that person. No substitute can be added. What should be considered when resolving this resource conflict?',[
'Prioritize activities on the original critical path and delay all other activities by the same number of days.','Reschedule activities to meet resource constraints and analyze the effect on the critical path and total project duration.','Divide the specialist’s hours in half so both activities run concurrently while retaining their original finish dates.','First extend both activity durations, then require other activities to be compressed to preserve the completion date.'
],'Resource leveling adjusts dates to reflect resource availability and may change the critical path or extend the project. Dates in the original network cannot automatically be assumed feasible under resource constraints.',[
'The critical path can inform priorities, but dependencies and float also need analysis; a uniform delay is not justified.',null,'Dividing time does not increase capacity, so the original durations may no longer be achievable.','Assess a realistic resource schedule first rather than assuming, without analysis, that other activities can be compressed to compensate.'
]);
put('a063','Reserve management','Advanced','An identified risk occurs, and the team plans to use the contingency reserve allocated to it. What should the project manager do?',[
'Request an equivalent amount of management reserve first and preserve the entire contingency reserve for future risks.','Immediately pay all related costs from the contingency reserve and update risk and cost records at month-end.','Activate the contingency reserve and response within approved authority, and promptly update cost forecasts and relevant records.','Add the entire contingency reserve to a new cost baseline so this risk no longer shows a variance.'
],'Contingency reserves address identified risks, but their use must still follow organizational and project authorization rules. Update the remaining reserve and cost forecast after use.',[
'A reserve already exists for this risk and should be used under its authorization; switching to a different reserve requires justification.','The response should be timely, but payment scope and authority still require checking. An available reserve does not replace controls.',null,'Contingency reserves are normally already included in the relevant budget arrangements. Do not count them twice or adjust the baseline to conceal actual performance.'
]);
put('a064','Three-point estimating','Advanced','An activity has an optimistic estimate of 4 days, a most likely estimate of 7 days, and a pessimistic estimate of 16 days. What is the PERT weighted estimate?',[
'9 days.','7 days.','10 days.','8 days.'
],'The common PERT estimate is (optimistic + 4 × most likely + pessimistic) / 6: (4 + 28 + 16) / 6 = 8 days. Its weighting differs from a simple average of the three estimates.',[
'This is the simple average of the three estimates; the question specifies PERT, which gives greater weight to the most likely estimate.','Using only the most likely estimate ignores the optimistic and pessimistic information.','This averages the optimistic and pessimistic estimates and ignores the fourfold weight on the most likely estimate.',null
]);
put('a065','Risk data quality','Advanced','A team uses risk probabilities from a previous project to assess a new one, although the countries and supply chain environments differ substantially. What is the most reasonable action?',[
'Assess whether historical probabilities are comparable with current conditions, validate assumptions, and calibrate the assessment with new information.','Keep the old probabilities as a fixed baseline and adjust only potential financial losses to current levels.','Ask the most experienced member to independently estimate new probabilities, replacing the historical data entirely with personal judgment.','Combine more historical projects to increase the sample size and use their average probabilities for the new project.'
],'Historical data can inform the assessment, but different conditions can change both probability and impact. Checking data quality and comparability prevents precise numbers from creating false confidence.',[
null,'Environmental differences can affect both probability and loss; changing only the financial impact is insufficient.','Expert judgment is useful, but it should be combined with an assessment of data quality and context rather than replacing all data without analysis.','A larger sample is not necessarily more relevant. Combining incomparable contexts can still introduce systematic bias.'
]);
put('a066','Quality sampling','Advanced','A production batch is large, and destructive testing cannot cover every item. The quality lead recommends sampling. What is the most important prerequisite?',[
'Use the supplier’s sample size from the previous batch so results are easy to compare.','Design a sampling plan aligned with risk and quality requirements, and explain the limitations of its statistical conclusions.','Prefer items produced during the most stable periods to reduce the effect of occasional variation.','Set the testing budget first and use the largest affordable sample as the basis for acceptable quality.'
],'Sampling balances cost and information. It requires a sound sample design and consideration of risk tolerance. Passing a sample does not guarantee that every item conforms; the plan must support the requirements.',[
'Batch conditions and risks can change. Reusing a sample size requires justification beyond comparability.',null,'Selectively excluding unstable periods biases the sample and prevents it from representing the whole batch.','Cost constraints matter, but statistical analysis and risk judgment are still needed to establish whether sampling supports the quality requirements.'
]);
put('a067','Agile reviews','Foundation','At an iteration review, the team shows only completed slides, demonstrates no working product, and invites no users. What is the best improvement?',[
'Invite the customer to the status presentation and emphasize percentage complete and commitments for the next iteration.','Ask the team to record a product demonstration, send it to users afterward, and treat no response as acceptance.','Demonstrate a working increment, invite relevant stakeholders to inspect it, and use feedback to adapt the backlog.','Replace the review with an internal quality inspection and organize user trials once the whole product is stable.'
],'A review creates value by inspecting product progress and obtaining feedback. A status presentation cannot adequately replace inspection and adaptation based on a real increment.',[
'Customer attendance alone is insufficient. Without inspecting the actual product, useful feedback on value and usability remains difficult.','A recording can support inspection, but treating silence as acceptance does not provide two-way feedback or appropriate acceptance.',null,'Internal quality checks do not replace continuous user feedback on value. Waiting for overall stability delays learning.'
]);
put('a068','Retrospective improvements','Foundation','Every retrospective produces more than a dozen suggestions, but nothing has changed by the next retrospective. What is the most effective adjustment?',[
'Commit to all suggestions in the next iteration so the team immediately acts on every problem.','Have the project manager select the easiest suggestions to implement and report the results to the team at month-end.','Create a detailed improvement register, assign each suggestion a number, and implement them when time is available.','Select a few high-priority improvements, define ownership and how results will be evaluated, and track their actual impact.'
],'Improvement must become part of real work instead of remaining a list. Limiting the number of actions and defining responsibilities and evaluation criteria creates a sustainable feedback loop.',[
'Committing to everything can exceed capacity and divide attention, making implementation less likely.','The easiest actions may not be the most valuable, and the team still lacks shared priorities and an evaluation process.','Numbering and recording suggestions improves organization, but without ownership and implementation dates, action can remain indefinitely deferred.',null
]);
put('a069','Dependencies and release','Advanced','A software feature is complete, but release depends on an external data license that has not yet been approved. How should the project manager report this?',[
'Clearly distinguish development completion from release readiness, identify the license owner, and report the expected delivery impact.','Show development as green and list the license separately as an external risk in a report appendix.','Suspend all project work until the license is approved to avoid further investment in an unreleasable product.','Ask the supplier to commit to a license approval date and substitute that commitment for the current release forecast.'
],'Status should reflect all conditions needed for delivery. Technical completion does not eliminate an external release dependency; report it transparently and manage it actively.',[
null,'Separate records can help, but a main status implying delivery readiness would understate an existing release blocker.','The licensing blocker needs management, but whether to suspend all work requires assessment. It does not automatically make every further investment worthless.','A third party’s commitment still requires validation of reliability and authority. An unapproved license cannot be treated as certain.'
]);
put('a070','Clarifying requirements','Foundation','A user story says, “The system must be very fast,” which gives the team no testable criterion. What is the best next step?',[
'Use competitor response times as a reference and directly adopt the industry average as the project standard.','Clarify use cases, load, and response expectations with business stakeholders and jointly define measurable acceptance criteria.','Have the technical lead propose an achievable response time and ask the business to confirm it after development.','Break the performance goal into development tasks and treat completion of all tasks as proof that the requirement is met.'
],'Vague adjectives do not establish a shared completion standard. Clarify the scenario, load, and response target to turn expectations into verifiable requirements.',[
'Industry data can inform the discussion, but it cannot replace confirmation of this product’s users, loads, and business scenarios.',null,'Technical feasibility matters, but acceptance criteria should be clarified together early rather than discussed only after implementation.','Completing activities does not prove that performance meets the need; measurable outcome criteria are still missing.'
]);
put('a071','Decision records','Foundation','Three months ago, the team selected a technical solution after comparing trade-offs. New members do not know why it was chosen and are repeating the same debate. What would help most?',[
'Ask the original decision-maker to explain it once and pass the rationale on verbally during future onboarding.','Make the selected solution a mandatory standard to reduce time spent revisiting the discussion.','Record the decision context, options, and assumptions so people can understand it and revisit it when conditions change.','Keep the original meeting recording for new members without separately documenting the trade-offs or rationale.'
],'A decision record preserves the context and trade-offs behind a choice. It reduces repeated debate and helps identify when a decision needs to change.',[
'Verbal explanations help, but without a durable, searchable record, effort may be repeated and information distorted.','A standard may reduce disagreement, but without context and assumptions it can lock in a choice that is no longer appropriate.',null,'A recording preserves evidence, but is difficult to search. Important decision reasoning should also become usable knowledge.'
]);
put('a072','Issue prioritization','Advanced','An issue log contains twenty unresolved items. One will block a critical acceptance event in two days, while several others concern page styling. How should the responsible person prioritize them?',[
'Resolve styling issues first to improve the closure rate and rebuild confidence before tackling the acceptance blocker.','Have each functional lead select one departmental issue and address them concurrently to distribute resources fairly.','Move every resource to the acceptance blocker and defer all other issues until after project closure without further analysis.','Sequence work using the effect on critical acceptance, urgency, work dependencies, and resolution cost.'
],'The number of closed issues is not the only measure. An issue that will soon block critical acceptance normally deserves priority, but resources should still be allocated using transparent criteria.',[
'Quick wins can encourage a team, but should not delay an issue that will soon block critical acceptance.','Equal departmental allocation may not reflect overall impact and urgency; protect critical outcomes first.','The blocker deserves priority, but resource allocation and deferring other issues still require analysis rather than an unconditional blanket decision.',null
]);
put('a073','Change impact analysis','Advanced','A team proposes a new algorithm to reduce computing costs. It will change how results are explained and could affect user training and audits. What is most needed before approval?',[
'Involve operations and compliance roles to assess the overall technical, training, and audit benefits and risks.','Complete a performance trial first and seek operations and audit input only after demonstrating lower computing costs.','Ask the algorithm developers to write explanatory documentation and use its completeness in place of audit and training assessments.','Ask finance to calculate the payback period for operating savings and adopt whichever option has the shortest period.'
],'A technical optimization can affect broader use and governance. A complete impact analysis includes dependencies such as training and auditing, rather than comparing running costs alone.',[
null,'A performance trial is useful, but effects on interpretability belong in the preapproval assessment rather than being considered only after technical success.','Developer documentation supports analysis but cannot replace evaluation by actual users and compliance roles.','Financial payback alone omits potential audit, training, and operational risks and cannot determine the decision.'
]);
put('a074','Delivery forecasting','Advanced','Throughput has varied substantially across the team’s last six iterations. Business stakeholders want to know when 60 backlog items will be complete. What is the most reasonable forecast?',[
'Use average throughput from the six iterations to calculate one date and make it a fixed commitment.','Use historical throughput and current conditions to provide a forecast range with a confidence explanation, and update it regularly.','Use the slowest iteration to calculate a deadline and treat the extra buffer as a guarantee that needs no further updates.','Have the team reestimate every item in precise hours, add the estimates, and directly convert the total into a finish date.'
],'Variation means a single certain date would conceal uncertainty. A data-based range supports risk-informed decisions, with assumptions about scope and team conditions made explicit.',[
'An average can inform a forecast, but a single-date commitment hides uncertainty when variation is substantial.',null,'A conservative estimate is still not a guarantee. Changes in scope, team, and environment require ongoing updates.','Detailed estimates do not automatically eliminate variability, dependencies, or capacity constraints. Simply adding them can create false precision.'
]);
put('a075','Business case','Foundation','A project is progressing to plan, but market changes have reduced expected benefits by 70%. What should the project manager do?',[
'First cut project costs to offset the reduced benefits, then evaluate business results after delivering the original scope.','Maintain the existing plan and record the benefit change as an external risk for analysis at closure.','Explain the market change to the sponsor and reassess the business case and justification for further investment.','Expand product scope to find new revenue and have the team start exploring features that could recover the loss.'
],'A project exists to create expected value, not merely to finish its original plan. Major environmental changes should trigger value reassessment, with the appropriate governance authority deciding whether to continue, adapt, or terminate.',[
'Cost reduction may be an option, but value should be reassessed first rather than assuming the original project remains worthwhile.','A major change in value affects current investment decisions and should not wait until closure.',null,'Additional scope also requires value validation and authorization; do not invest more resources before reassessing.'
]);
put('a076','Benefits realization','Foundation','A system has gone live, but the expected reduction in processing time has not appeared. What should the project lead focus on?',[
'Recheck technical acceptance and, once functionality passes, transfer the benefit problem entirely to operations.','Extend the observation period and wait for employees to become familiar with the system before considering corrective action.','Add automation features to reduce system operation time further, then measure business benefits again.','Work with the benefits owner to examine adoption, supporting processes, and benefit measures, and jointly define corrective actions.'
],'Delivering a system is an output; improved processing efficiency is a benefit. Confirm that users adopt it and supporting processes are in place, then continue tracking under the agreed responsibilities.',[
'Technical checks do not explain adoption or process barriers. Benefit handover also needs explicit accountability and joint analysis.','A reasonable time lag may matter, but examine the known gap instead of assuming that time alone will solve it.','Adding features without identifying the bottleneck may continue solving the wrong problem.',null
]);
put('a077','Benefits ownership','Advanced','After project delivery, the operations department will realize benefits over the next two years. What governance arrangement matters most before closure?',[
'Identify the operational benefits owner, measures, and measurement frequency, and transfer responsibility for ongoing tracking.','Document benefit targets in the closure report and have the project manager organize a review in two years.','Have finance calculate benefits at each year-end and use financial statements instead of ongoing benefits tracking.','Treat operations acceptance of deliverables as proof of realized benefits and discontinue project measures after closure.'
],'Someone must remain accountable for tracking benefits and taking action after the project team disbands. Clear ownership and measurement prevent post-delivery value from becoming unmanaged.',[
null,'Recording targets is insufficient; ongoing responsibility belongs with a role that has the operational resources and authority.','Financial validation helps, but cannot by itself manage adoption and process changes, and feedback may arrive too late.','Acceptance of an output does not mean future benefits have already occurred. Ownership and measurement are still needed.'
]);
put('a078','Governance authority','Foundation','A team identifies a decision that could change the company’s operating strategy in a region and exceeds project authority. What should it do?',[
'Choose the best option through a team review, then notify governance of the decision already reached by consensus.','Document strategic impacts and alternatives, and submit them to the organizational governance body with the necessary decision authority.','Split the decision into smaller project changes so each falls within existing authorization limits.','Keep the current plan and wait for the next portfolio review without proactively raising the uncertain strategic impact.'
],'The project team can analyze options and recommend action, but cannot change organizational strategy beyond its authority. Appropriate escalation places the decision with those accountable for it.',[
'The team can recommend an option, but consensus does not grant authority to decide organizational strategy.',null,'Splitting a decision does not change its overall strategic nature and must not be used to bypass authority limits.','Potentially significant impacts warrant timely analysis and escalation rather than passively waiting for a routine review.'
]);
put('a079','Regulatory changes','Advanced','Applicable data retention rules change during execution, and the current design may no longer comply. What should happen next?',[
'Change all retention settings using the strictest interpretation, then ask compliance to confirm the changes.','Require the supplier to guarantee compliance and transfer all design judgment and interpretation responsibility to it.','Confirm applicable requirements and design impacts with compliance experts, then assess and implement necessary changes through the appropriate controls.','Record the new rules as a risk and wait for the customer to submit a formal change request before adjusting the design.'
],'First establish which changed rules apply and what they require, then assess scope, cost, and risk. A project baseline does not justify ignoring applicable compliance obligations.',[
'Changing everything before confirming applicability can create conflicts with other retention obligations and business needs.','Supplier support helps, but the organization still needs to confirm applicability and meet its governance responsibilities.',null,'Applicable compliance requirements do not need to wait for a customer-initiated request.'
]);
put('a080','Data privacy','Foundation','A team plans to copy real customer records into a test environment to simplify troubleshooting. External personnel can access the environment, and data permissions have not been assessed. What is the best action?',[
'Restrict external personnel from downloading data but keep real customer records to preserve realistic testing.','Have external personnel sign confidentiality agreements, then copy all records directly into the environment.','Copy only the latest month of records and retain existing permissions because the data volume is smaller.','Confirm authorization and privacy requirements, use suitable de-identified or synthetic data, and limit access to what is necessary.'
],'Testing convenience does not replace data protection responsibilities. Minimize exposure, confirm purpose and permissions, and select protections suitable for the testing objective.',[
'Download restrictions provide only partial protection and do not confirm lawful authorization to use or access the real records in that environment.','A confidentiality agreement does not replace authorization, data minimization, or appropriate security controls.','Reducing volume helps, but the use of sensitive data and access to it still need to be lawful and appropriate.',null
]);
put('a081','Human oversight of AI','Advanced','A team wants generative AI to automatically approve high-value procurement contracts to save management time. The model has not been validated for this purpose. What should the lead do?',[
'Assess risks and controls, retain review by authorized people, and validate model capabilities in a low-risk scope.','Raise the model confidence threshold and automatically approve only contracts above that threshold.','Have legal approve a contract template, then let the model directly approve every contract that follows it.','Automatically approve contracts and have procurement sample them afterward, using the sampled error rate as the sole release criterion.'
],'High-impact decisions require appropriate accountability and validation. AI can assist analysis, but an unvalidated model should not automatically replace authorized people in major contract decisions.',[
null,'An unvalidated confidence score does not demonstrate reliability for major contracts or replace authorized accountability.','Template approval does not address amounts, changed terms, or case-specific business judgment. Automatic approval still needs validation and governance.','After-the-fact sampling cannot adequately control unvalidated high-value decisions; errors may create obligations before review.'
]);
put('a082','Validating AI output','Foundation','An AI tool generates a risk analysis containing industry statistics whose sources cannot be found. How should the team use it?',[
'Ask another AI model to review the conclusions and consider the data verified if both models agree.','Treat it as an unverified draft and check key facts, statistical sources, and conclusions before using it for decisions.','Remove unverifiable statistics but retain the risk priorities derived from them.','Label the statistics as estimates and submit the report directly, leaving management to judge credibility.'
],'Generative output may contain errors or invented citations. Important decisions should rely on verifiable information reviewed by accountable people.',[
'Models may share similar errors. Agreement between them does not replace verifiable primary evidence.',null,'If conclusions depend on unreliable data, removing the citations does not make those conclusions credible.','Stating uncertainty helps, but key verifiable facts should be checked first rather than transferring all basic validation to the recipient.'
]);
put('a083','AI bias','Advanced','An algorithm used to select employees for training consistently gives one group lower scores, and the data are not representative. How should the project respond?',[
'Improve overall model accuracy and examine differences between employee groups only after reaching the target.','Remove the group category field and continue using the model so it cannot see sensitive information.','Investigate model and data bias, assess effects on different groups, and establish correction and appeal mechanisms.','Arrange manual review only for affected employees while leaving the original model and data processing unchanged.'
],'Disparate impacts combined with unrepresentative data require analysis of bias and fairness risks. Accountable governance and feedback mechanisms are needed; overall accuracy is insufficient.',[
'Overall accuracy may conceal harm to a group. An identified fairness concern should not be postponed.','Other variables can act as proxies. Removing one field does not automatically remove bias; actual evaluation is needed.',null,'Manual review can provide interim protection but cannot replace diagnosis and correction of systemic bias.'
]);
put('a084','AI data governance','Foundation','A member is about to paste an unpublished customer contract into an external AI service using a personal account to summarize it. What should the lead do?',[
'Remove customer names and signatures first, then submit all remaining clauses through the personal AI account.','Obtain verbal agreement from the customer’s project contact and upload it to avoid internal approval delays.','Choose a service that says it does not train on user data, then process the contract directly through a personal account.','Check organizational policy, data processing terms, and authorization, then use an approved tool or an appropriate de-identification approach.'
],'Whether sensitive information may be shared with an external service depends on authorization and data protection arrangements. Efficiency does not replace checks on confidentiality, access, and retention rules.',[
'Removing names may leave confidential or identifiable contract information. Tool and usage authorization are still required.','The contact may lack authority to approve external disclosure, and verbal consent does not replace organizational requirements or a review of service terms.','Training policy is only one factor. Retention, access, security, and organizational approval also matter.',null
]);
put('a085','Sustainability trade-offs','Advanced','A project compares two devices. A is cheaper to buy but uses more energy. B costs more initially but lasts longer and uses less energy. What is the more reasonable evaluation?',[
'Compare life-cycle costs and environmental impacts under realistic usage scenarios, then choose against organizational objectives.','Select A based on the procurement budget and treat long-term energy costs as a separate operations expense.','Choose B because longer life and lower energy consumption are always more economical under any usage conditions.','Calculate only the payback period from electricity savings, excluding maintenance, disposal, and other environmental impacts.'
],'Sustainability decisions consider long-term operations, maintenance, and environmental effects. Initial price is one dimension; actual usage conditions and business objectives also shape the choice.',[
null,'Departmental budget boundaries do not replace life-cycle value. Ignoring operating costs can create local optimization at the expense of the whole.','The advantages still need validation against utilization, service life, and the price difference. No option is automatically best in every context.','Payback is useful evidence, but a narrow calculation can omit significant life-cycle factors.'
]);
put('a086','Sustainability measures','Foundation','A project claims it will reduce energy consumption but has defined neither current consumption nor measurement boundaries. What should be established first?',[
'Set a 20% reduction target, then choose an appropriate comparison period from later data.','Establish an actual consumption baseline, measurement scope, energy reduction targets, and a reliable data collection method.','Use published industry average consumption as the baseline to save project measurement costs.','Record new equipment’s rated power and use the difference between old and new ratings as actual energy savings.'
],'Without a comparable baseline and clear boundaries, genuine reductions are difficult to establish. Measures should avoid misleading effects from factors such as changing business volume.',[
'A target needs a credible baseline. Choosing the comparison period afterward risks selective conclusions.',null,'Industry data may not be comparable and cannot represent the project’s actual baseline without appropriate calibration.','Rated specifications differ from actual consumption. Operating hours, load, and measurement boundaries must also be established.'
]);
put('a087','Supply chain responsibility','Advanced','A reliable review finds that a competitively priced supplier violates organizational labor standards. How should the procurement project respond?',[
'Negotiate a price reduction as compensation and proceed after incorporating a risk premium into procurement costs.','Request a written assurance from the supplier, then retain the original selection and delivery plan once it arrives.','Use supplier due diligence and organizational standards to evaluate remediation or alternatives, and retain decision records.','Immediately select the second-ranked supplier without assessing remediation or the effects of replacement.'
],'A price advantage does not automatically offset explicit organizational responsibility standards. Verify facts and follow governance, considering feasible remediation, risk, and business continuity.',[
'Price compensation cannot automatically replace labor standards or necessary corrective action.','A written commitment may form part of the response, but credible findings require verification of remediation and control effectiveness.',null,'Replacement may be reasonable, but governance should assess actual risks, feasible remediation, and business continuity.'
]);
put('a088','Organizational change adoption','Foundation','A new system is fully functional, but employees continue using old spreadsheets and business benefits have not materialized. What should be analyzed next?',[
'Remove access to the old spreadsheets and use a mandatory cutover to establish new-system habits.','Increase functional training hours and define adoption success as every employee passing an operating test.','Publish departmental login rankings to managers and use competition to increase usage numbers.','Examine adoption barriers in real work and whether training, processes, and incentives support the new way of working.'
],'Technical delivery does not automatically change behavior. Understand why the old approach persists and align processes, capability, and incentives with the desired future state.',[
'A forced cutover may leave usability and process barriers unresolved and could disrupt business continuity.','Operating skills are only one part of adoption. Actual workflows, incentives, and outcomes also need examination.','Login counts do not demonstrate effective use or benefits realization, and rankings may encourage superficial activity.',null
]);
put('a089','Value versus outputs','Foundation','A team delivers twenty features in a month, but retention of key customers does not improve. Which measure would better indicate whether the product achieves its objective?',[
'Track actual changes in retention among target customers and analyze feature usage alongside related business outcomes.','Track monthly released feature counts and team velocity to confirm improving delivery capability.','Count total page clicks across all customers and use higher engagement as evidence of value to key customers.','Compare development cost per feature to demonstrate that the same budget produces more deliverables.'
],'Feature count is an output measure; retention is a target outcome. Examine whether delivery changed target user behavior while considering other influencing factors.',[
null,'Delivery capability is a process signal and does not directly establish whether customers stay.','Total clicks can mask loss of target customers and may not have a sufficient relationship with retention.','Cost-efficient delivery does not replace measurement of product business outcomes.'
]);
put('a090','Minimum viable product','Advanced','A team is preparing to spend six months developing a service but does not know whether customers will pay for its core feature. What is the best next step?',[
'Show prospective customers a complete feature list and base investment on the proportion who say they like it.','Design a minimum viable experiment to test the core willingness-to-pay assumption and agree on success criteria in advance.','Develop the three easiest features first and use an internal demonstration to prove the service is viable.','Collect competitor prices and start development as planned if comparable prices already exist in the market.'
],'The largest uncertainty is willingness to pay, not the ability to build every feature. A small experiment can produce decision evidence at lower cost.',[
'Stated interest does not equal payment behavior. The experiment should directly test the critical willingness-to-pay assumption.',null,'Easy development and internal technical feasibility may not validate the core value customers would pay for.','Competitor information helps, but does not prove that target customers will pay for this service’s core value.'
]);
put('a091','Sunk costs','Advanced','A project has already consumed substantial funding, but a credible new forecast shows that the incremental cost of continuing greatly exceeds incremental benefits. What is the most reasonable decision basis?',[
'Add prior spending to the benefit target for continued investment so the original investment can eventually break even.','Continue as long as remaining costs are below the original total budget to avoid leaving an unfinished project.','Compare the future costs and benefits of continuing versus exiting, including contractual obligations and strategic effects.','Terminate immediately to stop losses without spending time analyzing exit costs or strategic consequences.'
],'Past spending that cannot be recovered is a sunk cost. The decision should focus on the future, including exit obligations and strategic factors, rather than trying to justify prior spending.',[
'Increasing a target does not recover past spending. Future decisions still need credible incremental value.','The relationship between remaining costs and the original budget does not establish whether future investment is worthwhile.',null,'Termination may be appropriate, but exit costs, obligations, and strategic effects remain relevant future factors.'
]);
put('a092','Strategic alignment','Foundation','The organization shifts its strategy from rapid expansion to improving service for existing customers. A project still focuses on entering new markets. What should the project manager do?',[
'Add a customer service module while retaining every expansion objective to cover both old and new strategies.','Leave the project unchanged and assign the new strategy exclusively to future projects.','Rename the project and revise reporting measures, then evaluate new-strategy benefits after delivery.','Revisit objectives, priorities, and the business case with the sponsor to confirm alignment with the current strategy.'
],'A strategic change may alter the project’s relative value. The project manager should promptly provide impact analysis and support governance decisions instead of assuming original objectives remain valid indefinitely.',[
'Adding scope may dilute value and requires authorization. First assess whether the investment and objectives remain justified.','Projects already underway also need strategic alignment and are not automatically exempt from changes in organizational direction.','Renaming and changing measures do not replace reassessment of actual objectives and the business case.',null
]);
put('a093','Regulation and innovation','Advanced','A team wants to rapidly test a financial product using real customer funds, and regulatory requirements apply. What is the most reasonable approach?',[
'Co-design a controlled experiment with compliance and business stakeholders, defining authorized boundaries and customer protections.','Limit the experiment to a small number of customers and use real funds once each customer accepts the risk.','Apply strict technical transaction limits and complete compliance review after the experiment succeeds.','Have an external partner run the experiment and contractually transfer all regulatory responsibility to that partner.'
],'Rapid learning must occur within applicable boundaries. Controlled environments, limited scope, and authorization can support innovation; agility does not justify bypassing regulation.',[
null,'Customer consent and a small sample do not necessarily satisfy regulatory or organizational authorization requirements.','Limits may be useful controls, but cannot turn required prior review into an after-the-fact step.','Outsourcing does not automatically remove organizational responsibilities to customers and regulators. Appropriate governance remains necessary.'
]);
put('a094','Escalating business risks','Advanced','A team discovers that exchange-rate volatility could make an entire enterprise product line unprofitable. The exposure exceeds one project’s authority. What should happen?',[
'Assign high priority and enlarge the project reserve to cover product-line losses with project funds.','Escalate the organizational risk to an appropriate owner while continuing to manage specific project impacts.','Send a risk report to finance and close the project record to avoid duplicate accountability.','Recommend that procurement immediately lock all foreign-currency payments at the current exchange rate as a uniform response.'
],'Risks beyond project scope and authority belong with the appropriate organizational level. The project manager still manages project dependencies, forecasts, and interfaces to the response.',[
'Organization-wide exposure exceeds project authority and resources and requires ownership at the appropriate level.',null,'Transferring higher-level responsibility does not eliminate the need to track specific project effects and interfaces.','Hedging and similar strategies require appropriate expertise and organizational authority; a project cannot decide them unilaterally.'
]);
put('a095','Transparency and integrity','Foundation','A sponsor wants unfinished work reported as complete to show green status to the board. How should the project manager respond?',[
'Report that the project is on track and list unfinished work in an appendix to accommodate both needs.','Delay the report until the team catches up, then explain the overall delivery result to the board.','Report actual completion and impacts truthfully, offer recovery options, and follow communication governance.','Substitute forecast completion percentages for actual percentages and correct the difference in the next report.'
],'Accurate, transparent information enables effective decisions. Even under pressure, do not misstate completion. Objective facts and response options support a constructive resolution.',[
'Appendix disclosure does not correct a misleading headline status; core reporting must reflect actual completion.','Delaying information can deny governance timely decisions. Waiting for good news does not replace transparency.',null,'Forecasts can be shown separately, but must not replace actual data or mislead readers about current status.'
]);
put('a096','Conflicts of interest','Foundation','An evaluation committee member discovers that a close relative owns shares in a bidding supplier. What is the best action?',[
'Continue technical scoring but avoid price scoring, relying on professional judgment to reduce the relationship’s influence.','Request additional compliance evidence from that supplier and use stricter scrutiny to demonstrate impartiality.','Document every scoring rationale and disclose the relationship to the procurement lead after the evaluation.','Disclose the relationship and potential conflict in advance, then recuse from evaluation or apply prescribed controls under procurement policy.'
],'A conflict of interest can impair objectivity or create an appearance of unfairness. Disclosure and appropriate recusal protect procurement integrity better than personal assurances.',[
'Partial recusal may be permitted, but first disclose the conflict and let the appropriate process determine controls.','Stricter scrutiny does not replace disclosure and may itself create unfairness.','Documentation is useful, but advance disclosure allows timely controls.',null
]);
put('a097','Benefit assumptions','Advanced','A business case assumes automation will save the labor cost of five positions, but operations plans to reassign those employees without reducing headcount. How should the value analysis change?',[
'Distinguish cash savings, freed capacity, and other benefits, and recalculate value using the actual realization mechanisms.','Count freed hours multiplied by wage rates as cash savings because employees genuinely spend less time entering data.','Convert all five salaries into additional revenue, assuming reassigned employees generate an equal amount.','Remove all labor-related benefits and use only incurred software procurement costs as the basis of value.'
],'Reassignment may create capacity and service value without reducing cash expenditure. Classify and measure benefits according to how they are actually realized to avoid double counting or exaggeration.',[
null,'Less time spent on one activity does not necessarily reduce payroll spending and cannot automatically be counted as cash savings.','Reassignment can create value, but salary costs cannot be converted directly into revenue without evidence.','Unchanged headcount does not eliminate capacity or service benefits. Classify and validate them rather than deleting them entirely.'
]);
put('a098','Monitoring the external environment','Foundation','A competitor launches a low-price substitute, and an expensive planned feature may no longer offer a market advantage. What should the team do?',[
'Accelerate the feature to retain the competitive benefits in the original business case through earlier release.','Analyze value and priority impacts with business and product owners, then adapt the plan through governance.','Reduce development cost and retain the original priority as long as the original profit margin can be maintained.','Immediately remove the feature and allocate its budget to other market opportunities that have not yet been analyzed.'
],'External changes should inform ongoing value assessment. Whether to retain a feature depends on new business evidence, not how long it has been in the plan.',[
'Acceleration may be an option, but the changed competitive position requires validation of benefit assumptions first.',null,'Cost improvement may not restore customer value or market differentiation; an overall reassessment is needed.','Cancellation may be reasonable, but still requires value and dependency analysis rather than an immediate budget decision based only on competitor news.'
]);
put('a099','Business continuity','Advanced','A system cutover requires downtime, and any lengthy interruption during peak business periods would cause major losses. What must be confirmed before the go-live decision?',[
'Choose a time when the technical team is least busy and add on-call staff to address failures.','Fix the launch time after the final performance test and let the on-site team decide rollback conditions during cutover.','Confirm a business-acceptable cutover window, rollback criteria, continuity arrangements, and decision authority.','Obtain business approval for downtime, then have operations prepare the recovery plan on cutover day.'
],'Passing technical tests does not by itself ensure a safe cutover. Define an acceptable downtime window and a recovery path if it fails to protect critical operations.',[
'Staff convenience does not establish acceptable business downtime, and rollback and authority arrangements are still missing.','Tests cannot cover every cutover risk. Rollback criteria and responsibilities should be clear in advance.',null,'Downtime approval matters, but recovery and continuity arrangements should be prepared and confirmed before the go-live decision.'
]);
put('a100','Measuring project success','Advanced','A project delivers on time and on budget, but customer satisfaction declines and expected business objectives are not achieved. What is the most reasonable assessment?',[
'Declare the project successful and attribute all unrealized benefits to operations without further analysis.','Treat lower customer satisfaction as proof that every aspect of project management failed, ignoring delivery measures.','First improve satisfaction survey scores and leave other business objectives to the next project.','Some delivery constraints were met, but the overall result still requires assessment of value, adoption, quality, and stakeholder outcomes.'
],'Time and cost are important constraints, but do not fully define success. Examine whether delivery produced expected business value and transparently analyze why outcomes were not achieved.',[
'Project and operations responsibilities should be distinguished, but that does not remove project value or handover from consideration.','Satisfaction matters but does not erase delivery efficiency. An overall assessment should distinguish achieved and unachieved objectives.','Higher satisfaction does not establish achievement of other objectives; committed business outcomes still require transparent tracking.',null
]);
Object.assign(window.PMP_EN.questions, questions);
const lessons = {};
const lesson = (id,title,summary,sections,takeaways,prompt,answer) => {
  lessons[id]={title,summary,sections:sections.map(([title,body])=>({title,body})),takeaways,scenario:{prompt,answer}};
};
lesson('l01','Build a project manager’s decision-making mindset','Move from completing tasks to delivering value, and establish a sequence for judging situational questions.',[
['Why projects exist','A project is a temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique result. A completed system, building, or event is an output; actual adoption, lower costs, or improved service are outcomes. A project manager attends to both delivery constraints and business purpose. A product launched on schedule but used by nobody may still fail to realize its expected value.'],
['Understand the situation before acting','First identify the life cycle, the role’s authority, whether the issue has already occurred, and whether the question asks what to do first or ultimately. When information is insufficient, understand the facts and effects. When a safety or compliance risk is already clear, take timely action under the applicable rules. “Analyze first” is not a universal rule that excuses delaying urgent action.'],
['Combine collaboration with accountability','The project manager facilitates joint problem-solving and helps remove impediments while remaining responsible for transparent communication, integrated decisions, and appropriate escalation. Respecting team expertise does not mean leaving problems unmanaged; escalation to a sponsor does not mean passing away responsibility. Escalation is appropriate when an issue exceeds authority or reaches a governance threshold.'],
['Turn study into judgment practice','For every question, review the objective, constraints, and best action at that moment. Record error causes, such as confusing agile and predictive approaches, missing the word “first,” or making commitments beyond authority. Explaining why the other choices are unsuitable helps you apply knowledge to new situations more effectively than memorizing answers.']
],['Completing outputs does not equal realizing value.','“First” tests action sequence; “best” tests overall suitability.','Use collaboration, evidence, and authority boundaries rather than rigid answer slogans.'],
'A project finishes within budget, but customer usage is low. Should the project manager report only delivery success?',
'No. Work with the business owner to examine adoption, expected benefits, and usage barriers, and clarify accountability for improvements and benefits tracking. Measure delivery performance and business outcomes separately.');
lesson('l02','Predictive, agile, and hybrid: choose the right approach','Choose a life cycle based on requirements stability, feedback speed, and compliance constraints.',[
['Where predictive approaches fit','When requirements are relatively clear, change is costly, and major dependencies need early coordination, predictive approaches improve predictability through early overall planning and baseline control. Plans still evolve, but baseline changes follow agreed approval processes. Predictive does not mean rejecting learning or knowing every detail from day one.'],
['How agile reduces uncertainty','Agile uses short feedback cycles, small batches, and continuous prioritization so the team can adapt as real information becomes available. Iteration improves a solution progressively; incremental development adds usable functionality progressively. Both can happen together. Adaptive approaches still need objectives, budgets, quality requirements, and visible working agreements.'],
['Designing hybrid interfaces','A hybrid approach manages different parts of the work in suitable ways: for example, equipment installation through a milestone plan and software through iterative delivery. Define the interfaces early: when requirements freeze, how increments are accepted, how cross-team dependencies are coordinated, and who may approve changes. Merely running two kinds of meetings does not create an effective hybrid approach.'],
['Tailoring is not arbitrary omission','Select practices using organizational policy, risk, team experience, and delivery characteristics, and explain the rationale. Establish essential governance and quality requirements first, then adapt the process over time. Regulatory evidence, contractual commitments, and safety controls cannot be removed merely by calling the work agile. Their implementation may change, but the actual requirements must still be met.']
],['Uncertain requirements call for earlier and more frequent feedback.','Hybrid approaches must manage interfaces between different delivery rhythms.','Tailoring needs a rationale and must preserve necessary governance.'],
'A hospital project purchases fixed-specification equipment while developing appointment software whose requirements are still being explored. How could the work be organized?',
'Use predictive procurement and installation planning for the equipment and iterations to validate software user needs. Coordinate interface testing, privacy review, and the overall launch through shared milestones.');
lesson('l03','Stakeholders: move from a list to meaningful engagement','Identify people who influence or are affected by the project, and understand their interests, influence, and participation needs.',[
['Look beyond the organization chart','Stakeholders may influence a project or be affected by it, including customers, users, team members, regulators, operations staff, suppliers, and communities. Identification continues throughout the project. A new regulation, supplier, or delivery scope may reveal groups that were previously overlooked.'],
['Analyze interests and influence','Besides power and interest, understand positions, knowledge, influence networks, expected benefits, and potential losses. A frontline expert with little formal seniority can strongly affect actual adoption. Classification tools help choose engagement approaches; they should not become permanent labels. Reassess as circumstances change.'],
['Make the engagement plan actionable','Describe current and desired engagement, then design specific interactions. Operations representatives might help define acceptance criteria early, or affected departments might attend pilot demonstrations. One-way reports rarely resolve resistance by themselves. Provide listening, negotiation, and feedback loops so people can see how their input affects decisions.'],
['Measure engagement effectiveness','Look at whether key decisions occur on time, feedback is used, and conflict decreases, rather than simply counting meetings. When people oppose the project, understand legitimate concerns and competing interests before developing solutions together. Excluding opponents may temporarily reduce debate but increase later acceptance and adoption risks.']
],['Stakeholder identification is continuous.','Understand resistance before designing targeted engagement.','Engagement is different from the frequency of receiving information.'],
'A system pilot goes well, but operations refuses formal handover. What should the project manager do first?',
'Ask operations representatives about their concerns, check operational capability, support processes, and acceptance criteria, then jointly plan remediation and transition. Do not rely only on asking the sponsor to force acceptance.');
lesson('l04','Communication: turn information into shared understanding','Choose content, channels, and cadence for each audience, and confirm that information has actually been understood.',[
['Communication needs come before tools','Identify who needs which information, why, when, and at what confidentiality level before choosing reports, boards, meetings, or a knowledge base. Executives typically need value, major risks, and decisions required; delivery teams need detailed dependencies and action items. Sending the same lengthy report to everyone does not ensure adequate communication.'],
['Choose interactive, push, and pull methods','Complex disputes benefit from real-time interaction that checks mutual understanding. Routine status can be pushed, with clear action requests. Large reference collections belong in a searchable knowledge base for people to pull as needed. Urgent matters should use agreed escalation channels rather than assuming everyone will promptly check group chat.'],
['Close the loop and preserve traceability','Record decisions, owners, and deadlines from important meetings. Use restatement or written confirmation for key agreements so “I sent it” is not mistaken for “they accepted it.” Keep necessary sources and versions in updated reports, and align terminology and metric definitions so teams do not debate the same problem using different data.'],
['Communicate across cultures and locations','Do not automatically interpret silence as agreement. Provide asynchronous input, clear agendas, and materials in advance for people in different time zones or with different language proficiency. Sensitive feedback is usually best discussed privately first; whole-team working rules should be agreed together. Adjust communication frequency based on effectiveness, not the assumption that more is always better.']
],['Understanding and action are evidence of successful communication.','Use two-way interaction for disputes and searchable records for knowledge.','Important decisions need an owner, deadline, and record.'],
'A customer does not reply to weekly reports for two weeks, then says they did not know a milestone was delayed. How should communication improve?',
'Confirm the customer’s information needs and preferred channels. Proactively discuss major deviations and check understanding. Weekly reports preserve evidence but do not replace timely communication about critical issues.');
lesson('l05','Servant leadership and team empowerment','Help teams make sound decisions through clear goals, support, and boundaries.',[
['Leadership starts by creating the right conditions','Servant leadership focuses on what the team needs to work effectively: available resources, timely removal of impediments, and the ability to raise concerns safely. The project manager does not need to decide every specialist’s technical details. Clarify goals, constraints, and priorities so expertise can translate into outcomes.'],
['Empowerment needs clear boundaries','Explain what the team may decide independently, what requires consultation, and what must be escalated. For example, the team may choose implementation methods but cannot independently promise compliance exemptions or contract price changes. Clear boundaries reduce waiting and repeated permission requests while preventing autonomy from being mistaken for an absence of accountability.'],
['Psychological safety and accountability','Encourage early reporting of errors, risks, and differing views. Discuss facts instead of attacking character. Psychological safety does not remove responsibility for commitments. The team still needs clear completion criteria, commitments, and feedback. Persistent problems require support, improvement plans, and appropriate follow-up rather than unlimited tolerance.'],
['Adapt support to maturity','New teams may need clearer guidance and demonstrations. Mature teams often need organizational impediments removed and space to act independently. Use one-to-one conversations to understand motivation and capability, and coaching questions to support growth. No single leadership style should be applied unchanged to every person and every crisis.']
],['Help remove impediments instead of taking over every decision.','Empowerment requires clear goals, boundaries, and accountability.','Safety in raising concerns can coexist with accountability for results.'],
'A team waits every day for the project manager to assign tasks, slowing delivery. How should this improve?',
'Clarify goals, priorities, and delegated authority with the team. Support members in organizing and sharing work, then regularly inspect impediments and outcomes. Simply withdrawing management or handing over all responsibility would be insufficient.');
lesson('l06','Conflict management: address differences without labeling people','Separate interests, facts, and positions, and resolve conflict at the appropriate level.',[
['Conflict can create value','Disagreements about technical solutions, resources, or priorities can expose important information. First assess the source, impact, and urgency of conflict. Avoid treating normal differences of opinion as attitude problems. Addressing small disagreements promptly can prevent personal hostility and cross-departmental distrust.'],
['Move from positions to shared interests','A proposed solution is a position; the outcome someone wants to protect is an interest. One person asking to delay and another demanding an on-time launch may respectively be protecting quality and a market window. Help them compare phased scope, pilots, or resource adjustments against shared criteria. This is more sustainable than demanding unconditional surrender by one side.'],
['Choose a strategy for the situation','Collaborative problem-solving often supports lasting relationships, but takes time. An urgent safety issue may require a direct decision by someone with authority. Temporary avoidance may allow emotions to settle or facts to be gathered, but should not evade a significant issue. Compromise means each party gives something up; it does not necessarily address the root cause.'],
['Escalate with a reason','Facilitate resolution at the lowest appropriate level first. Escalate through governance when the matter exceeds authority, involves serious misconduct, or persistently threatens objectives. Provide facts, impacts, attempted solutions, and the decision needed. Avoid demanding replacement of team members before understanding the issue, and avoid endless delay out of reluctance to escalate.']
],['Check facts and interests before choosing a response.','Collaboration is not the only answer to every urgent situation.','Escalation should include impacts and alternatives.'],
'Developers and testers blame each other for a delay. How should the project manager intervene first?',
'Help both groups use workflow evidence and facts to identify bottlenecks and confirm the shared delivery objective, then agree on improvements. Punishing either side before understanding the cause would intensify conflict.');
lesson('l07','Team development, coaching, and knowledge transfer','Turn capability gaps into actionable support and development plans.',[
['Working agreements create shared rules','Jointly agree on meeting behavior, decision methods, working hours, conflict handling, and completion standards. Agreements are more likely to be followed when members help create them. Revisit them when new members join or collaboration problems arise instead of permanently filing away the document from the first meeting.'],
['Diagnose performance problems first','Missed expectations may result from missing skills, unclear goals, insufficient resources, blocked dependencies, or personal difficulties. Understand the cause through a respectful one-to-one conversation, then choose training, pairing, work adjustments, or organizational support. Assuming every performance problem is a lack of motivation often misses the actual impediment.'],
['Coaching, mentoring, and training','Coaching uses questions to help people think and act. Mentoring offers experience-based guidance. Training develops specific knowledge or skills. These can be combined, but define the expected change and review date. Evaluate training by improvements in actual work, not merely attendance counts.'],
['Reduce dependence on key individuals','Use paired work, role rotation, shared documentation, and rehearsals to sustain knowledge transfer. Collect lessons continuously and apply them to later work; waiting until project closure is often too late. Before someone leaves, arrange handover and verify the recipient’s capability instead of merely copying a document to a shared folder.']
],['Create working agreements together and revisit them.','Capability, environmental, and motivational problems need different responses.','Knowledge transfer should verify that the recipient can perform independently.'],
'The only specialist who understands deployment will leave next month. What should the project manager arrange?',
'Arrange documentation, paired operation, and a deployment rehearsal led by the recipient. Check access permissions and backup ownership. Asking the specialist only to write instructions does not eliminate the single-person dependency.');
lesson('l08','Negotiation, resources, and distributed collaboration','Coordinate commitments under resource constraints and protect a sustainable pace.',[
['Resource commitments require negotiation','In a matrix organization, a project manager may lack direct staffing authority. Agree with functional managers on skills, allocation percentages, availability windows, and priorities, and make commitments visible. When critical resource conflicts arise, assess the impact and negotiate adjustments. Do not assume without authorization that people are always available full-time to the project.'],
['Preparation matters more than toughness','Clarify your objectives, minimum acceptable outcome, negotiable conditions, and alternatives, and understand the other party’s constraints. Negotiate around objective criteria and shared interests, distinguishing mandatory constraints from flexible preferences. Do not make delivery commitments you cannot meet merely to win a negotiation.'],
['Design remote collaboration explicitly','Distributed teams need agreed overlap hours, asynchronous response times, and traceable workflows. Rotate inconvenient meeting times so the same location does not always bear the burden. Use pairing or brief meetings for work requiring frequent collaboration, and protect uninterrupted time for deep work. Tools cannot replace clear agreements.'],
['Watch workload and inclusion','Overallocating people creates context-switching losses and quality risks. Sustained overtime also undermines delivery capability. Use capacity and actual throughput to discuss feasible commitments instead of relying on individual heroics to compensate for systemic problems. Giving members from different backgrounds opportunities to contribute and grow can also improve decision quality.']
],['Resource allocation needs explicit commitments and priorities.','Negotiate around interests, constraints, and feasible commitments.','A sustainable pace is part of delivery capability.'],
'Three projects each schedule the same security specialist full-time next week. What should happen?',
'Bring the relevant managers together to coordinate time using organizational priorities, risk, and dependencies. Adjust sequencing or add capability where necessary. Three separate plans cannot each be treated as confirmed resource commitments.');
lesson('l09','Scope, requirements, and acceptance','Turn vague expectations into verifiable requirements and control unassessed scope growth.',[
['Requirements must be verifiable','Use interviews, workshops, observation, or prototypes to understand real needs, distinguishing business needs, user needs, and preferences for particular solutions. Translate “the system must be fast” into response times and acceptable ranges under defined conditions. Agree on acceptance criteria early so the customer and team do not discover different definitions of success only after delivery.'],
['The predictive scope baseline','The scope statement describes boundaries and major deliverables. The work breakdown structure decomposes deliverables into manageable components, while the WBS dictionary provides details. Decomposition should cover the agreed scope without omissions or hidden unauthorized additions. A WBS is not a day-by-day schedule; they serve different purposes.'],
['Agile requirements and ordering','The product backlog evolves through learning. The product owner orders it using value, risk, dependencies, and feedback. Refinement makes upcoming work sufficiently clear; planning then selects work in light of capacity. Story points are relative estimates and should not directly become cross-team productivity comparisons or employee performance measures.'],
['Scope validation and quality inspection','Quality inspection examines whether deliverables meet quality requirements. Scope validation concerns formal acceptance by the customer or authorized party. They are related but distinct. When a customer requests something new, clarify its nature, impacts, and applicable process before prioritizing it or submitting a change. Do not promise it verbally and start immediately.']
],['Early acceptance criteria reduce later disputes.','A WBS organizes delivery scope; a schedule organizes work sequence and timing.','Requirement changes need controls appropriate to the life cycle.'],
'A customer says adding a report will take “only half a day.” Can the team immediately include it in the current delivery?',
'First verify the business need and effects on scope, testing, cost, and schedule, then use the change process or backlog ordering. A small effort estimate does not automatically permit bypassing authorization.');
lesson('l10','Schedule, critical path, and dependencies','Find the work that actually determines completion, then choose a suitable schedule compression method.',[
['Examine dependencies before dates','Identify activities, estimate durations and resources, and establish logical relationships. Distinguish mandatory dependencies, discretionary dependencies that may be adjusted, and external dependencies. Calendars, availability, and time zones can all affect the schedule. Assigning deadlines without analyzing sequencing makes delays difficult to explain.'],
['What the critical path means','For a given network and assumptions, the critical path is generally the longest path determining the earliest completion, with the least total float. A delay to a noncritical activity within its float may leave the project finish date unchanged, although resources or other constraints can still be affected. The critical path can change during execution and needs ongoing updates.'],
['The costs of compressing a schedule','Crashing usually trades additional suitable resources and cost for time; adding people cannot accelerate every task. Fast tracking overlaps work originally planned in sequence, increasing rework and coordination risks. Assess the critical path and consequences before choosing an action instead of prescribing overtime for everyone.'],
['Planning and forecasting in agile','Use historical throughput, capacity, and completion trends to forecast delivery scope or timing, and explain uncertainty. Velocity can help the same team plan but is not a contractual guarantee of future performance. Complex dependencies need active coordination and work splitting; daily meetings alone will not make blockers disappear.']
],['The critical path concerns the effect on project completion.','Crashing generally increases cost; fast tracking generally increases risk.','Forecasts need historical evidence and explicit assumptions.'],
'A noncritical task is two days late but has four days of total float. Should the project immediately be declared delayed?',
'Check logic, resource impacts, and float consumption first. This task’s delay alone does not prove an overall delay. Update the schedule and continue monitoring the remaining margin.');
lesson('l11','Cost and earned value: make numbers support decisions','Understand PV, EV, AC, and common measures; explain their business meaning before calculating.',[
['The three basic measures','PV is the budgeted value of work scheduled by the status date. EV is the budgeted value of work actually completed. AC is the actual cost of completing that work. Earned value is not sales revenue. Consistent measurement dates and completion rules allow plans, accomplishments, and spending to be compared on the same scale.'],
['Variances and efficiency','CV = EV − AC; a negative value indicates cost overrun. SV = EV − PV; a negative value indicates less work completed than planned in budgeted-value terms. CPI = EV / AC and SPI = EV / PV; values below 1 usually indicate unfavorable performance in the corresponding dimension. SV is not days of delay, and SPI does not replace critical path analysis. Do not calculate a ratio when its denominator is zero.'],
['State forecast assumptions','BAC is the budget at completion. If current cost efficiency is expected to continue, use EAC = BAC / CPI. If past variance is a one-time event and remaining work will follow its original budget, use EAC = AC + BAC − EV. ETC = EAC − AC, and VAC = BAC − EAC. The assumptions in the question determine the formula; memorizing just one is insufficient.'],
['Reserves and action','Contingency reserves support planned responses to identified risks. Management reserves address unforeseen work within scope, and their use follows authorization rules. When a variance appears, validate data, analyze trends and root causes, then evaluate corrective action. Changing the baseline to conceal an overrun damages transparency instead of improving performance.']
],['EV is the budgeted value of completed work.','CPI or SPI below 1 deserves attention, interpreted in context.','A forecast formula’s assumptions matter more than memorizing the formula alone.'],
'With PV = 100, EV = 80, and AC = 90, what is the project’s status?',
'CV = −10 and SV = −20; CPI is about 0.89 and SPI is 0.80. Cost efficiency is unfavorable and progress is behind in budgeted-value terms. Check the critical path as well; these figures do not directly tell you the number of days delayed.');
lesson('l12','Quality, completion standards, and continuous improvement','Build quality into the process and reduce reliance on a final inspection.',[
['Quality begins with clear requirements','Quality means meeting requirements and fitness for use, not simply more features or a higher grade. Define quality measures, test methods, acceptance conditions, and responsibilities, then incorporate them into planning. Features unsupported by requirements can add maintenance and failure risk. Even if the team considers them superior, they may be gold plating.'],
['Prevention is more dependable than rework','Training, reviews, automated checks, and process improvement are prevention or appraisal investments. Rework, refunds, and incident losses are failure costs. Appropriate early investment can reduce the total cost of quality. A large inspection just before delivery often cannot repair extensive defects introduced much earlier.'],
['Choose suitable analysis tools','A cause-and-effect diagram organizes potential causes. A Pareto chart helps identify categories accounting for the largest share of impact. A control chart helps assess process stability. Correlation does not prove causation, and tools do not automatically prescribe the right response. Understand data sources and process context before testing hypotheses.'],
['Built-in quality in agile','The Definition of Done establishes a shared quality threshold, while acceptance criteria describe expectations for a specific item. An increment must meet the Definition of Done; untested functionality cannot count as genuinely complete. Retrospectives identify improvements, select a few actionable experiments, assign owners, and evaluate results in later cycles.']
],['Define quality standards before work begins.','The Definition of Done and item-specific acceptance criteria complement each other.','Improvement must become an action whose results can be checked.'],
'To maintain velocity, a team marks untested features as done, and defects then surge. How should it respond?',
'Reaffirm and follow a Definition of Done that includes testing, diagnose testing bottlenecks, and improve the workflow. Inflated completion counts must not conceal unfinished work and quality risks.');
Object.assign(window.PMP_EN.lessons, lessons);
Object.assign(window.PMP_EN.weeks, {
1:{title:'Build your map and establish a starting point',focus:'Understand the updated exam, check eligibility and training arrangements, and establish a value-focused decision framework.',tasks:[
'Spend 2 hours on this week’s 3 lessons and map the differences between predictive, agile, and hybrid approaches.',
'Spend 1 hour checking eligibility and experience evidence, then schedule 3 hours of formal training.',
'Complete 20 diagnostic questions and 10 mixed practice questions; spend 2 hours explaining every error.',
'Spend 1 hour creating error tags and reserve another hour for revision or work conflicts.'
],checkpoint:'Explain project value, the three life cycles, and your eligibility preparation. Treat the diagnostic score only as a starting point.'},
2:{title:'Understand stakeholders and communication',focus:'Move from sending information to meaningful engagement and shared understanding.',tasks:[
'Spend 2 hours on the 2 lessons and draw a stakeholder map for one of your own projects.',
'Complete 4 hours of formal training. If training is already complete, use the time for assigned reference reading.',
'Spend 2 hours on 30–40 People-domain situational questions and analyze the action sequence in each.',
'Spend 1 hour reviewing week 1 errors and reserve up to 1 more hour for reflection or catching up.'
],checkpoint:'Choose communication methods for different stakeholders and explain why sending reports alone does not resolve resistance.'},
3:{title:'Lead teams and handle conflict',focus:'Practice servant leadership, authority boundaries, and collaborative problem-solving.',tasks:[
'Spend 2 hours on the 2 lessons and write one empowerment scenario and one conflict scenario.',
'Complete 4 hours of formal training. If already complete, check unfamiliar concepts and use reference reading.',
'Spend 2 hours on 30–40 People questions, comparing when collaboration, negotiation, and escalation apply.',
'Spend 1 hour retrying incorrect questions after a gap of at least 3 days; retain up to 1 hour of flexible time.'
],checkpoint:'When facing conflict, describe facts, interests, and authority before considering punishment or unconditional escalation.'},
4:{title:'Develop capability and define scope boundaries',focus:'Establish team agreements and turn vague requirements into verifiable deliverables.',tasks:[
'Spend 2 hours on the 3 lessons and collect examples of working agreements and acceptance criteria.',
'Complete 4 hours of formal training. If already complete, strengthen requirements and scope knowledge.',
'Spend 2 hours on 40 mixed practice questions and check weak People-domain topics.',
'Spend 1 hour summarizing error patterns from the first 4 weeks and reserve up to 1 hour to catch up. If behind, move the target date instead of removing review time.'
],checkpoint:'Distinguish requirements, scope, quality inspection, and formal acceptance, and explain early concepts without notes.'},
5:{title:'Master planning and delivery measures',focus:'Study the critical path, cost forecasts, and quality control.',tasks:[
'Spend 2.5 hours on the 3 lessons and calculate CPI, SPI, and EAC under both assumptions by hand.',
'Complete 4 hours of formal training. If already complete, use reference reading and draw a dependency network.',
'Spend 2 hours on 40 Process questions. For calculations, state the measure’s meaning before substituting values.',
'Spend 1 hour reviewing People-domain errors and retain 0.5 hours of flexible time.'
],checkpoint:'Explain the business meaning of schedule and cost measures without treating SV as days of delay.'},
6:{title:'Respond to risks and manage changes',focus:'Understand risk ownership, integrated impacts, and contractual boundaries.',tasks:[
'Spend 2 hours on the 3 lessons and write one risk response and one change-control workflow.',
'Complete 4 hours of formal training. If already complete, review how contract types allocate risk.',
'Spend 2 hours on 40 risk, change, and procurement questions, explaining the problem with each unsuitable option.',
'Spend 1 hour retrying week 5 errors and reserve up to 1 hour to close remaining gaps.'
],checkpoint:'Distinguish risks from issues and choose an appropriate change path based on authority and life cycle.'}
});
})();
